om/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/healthkey-ai/exact/commit/8a1caca9dce44505df5a1102532d37aad4f478fc","node_id":"C_kwDORYlqjNoAKDhhMWNhY2E5ZGNlNDQ1MDVkZjVhMTEwMjUzMmQzN2FhZDRmNDc4ZmM","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/healthkey-ai/exact/commit/0647513f7809eaa2f3ce9ad6538f910cf3e2ed19","sha":"0647513f7809eaa2f3ce9ad6538f910cf3e2ed19","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/healthkey-ai/exact/commits/0647513f7809eaa2f3ce9ad6538f910cf3e2ed19"},{"html_url":"https://github.com/healthkey-ai/exact/commit/86f5a7446f1f783c234a5dddffda9d87b4081061","sha":"86f5a7446f1f783c234a5dddffda9d87b4081061","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/healthkey-ai/exact/commits/86f5a7446f1f783c234a5dddffda9d87b4081061"}],"sha":"8a1caca9dce44505df5a1102532d37aad4f478fc","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/healthkey-ai/exact/commits/8a1caca9dce44505df5a1102532d37aad4f478fc"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/69346755?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/LeoMo42","id":69346755,"login":"LeoMo42","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjY5MzQ2NzU1","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/healthkey-ai/exact/commits/a8f8525f1bc5cc4fb74b516b08922e668152d1c0/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-15T15:29:02Z","email":"69346755+LeoMo42@users.noreply.github.com","name":"LeoMo42"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-15T15:29:02Z","email":"69346755+LeoMo42@users.noreply.github.com","name":"LeoMo42"},"message":"fix(security): the refusals that were not credentials, and the tests a stub passed\n\nA differential fuzzer over 11,155 inputs, with a C oracle linked against\nlibpq 17 deciding what actually carries a credential, found no way to get\na password back out of either half. The remaining defects are the other\ndirection — refusing DSNs that carry nothing, disagreeing about which\nthose are, and tests that could not tell.\n\n**Refusals of things that are not credentials.** An empty `password=` is\nnot a password; libpq parses none out of it and the Python twin says so in\nas many words. bash refused it — and a refusal under `set -euo pipefail`\naborts container start, on the realistic `?password=${SECRET}` with SECRET\nunset. Likewise `host=h options=''-c ?password=x''`: the positional rule had\nbeen applied to the authority guard and not to the two query guards, so a\n`?` inside a conninfo *value* was read as a query separator. And Python\nrefused `postgresql://u@h/dbname password=s3cr3t`, where libpq reads the\nwhole tail as the database name — its keyword scans now run for keyword\nconninfo only, which is what they are for.\n\n**A password that decoded correctly and was then truncated.** Command\nsubstitution eats trailing newlines, so `%0A` reached PGPASSWORD without\nit: the connection authenticates with a different password than libpq\nwould have used. A sentinel byte preserves it.\n\n**One more twin disagreement.** `?password%20=s3cr3t` is not a parameter\nlibpq accepts, but the secret is in argv either way — the standard this\nmodule already applies to `PassWord=`. bash refused it; Python did not.\n\n**And the tests.** Replacing psql_dsn.sh with a stub that returns an empty\nstring for every input passed every case in the twin-agreement class: it\nasserted the secret was absent, never that the DSN survived. It now\nasserts what came back, which no stub satisfies — 23 failures against that\nstub, from zero. The helper also read only the first line of the bash\noutput, so a DSN containing a newline hid the rest; the corpus had stopped\none whitespace character short of the input that exploits it, which is\nexactly the shape of the last two rounds'' bugs. Both fixed, and the corpus\nnow carries the newline case.\n\nThree comments were saying things that are not true: that the init scripts\nrefuse an unrecognised scheme (they refuse a credential, whatever the\nscheme), that the positional rule mirrors libpq (libpq''s test is\n`strncmp(\"postgresql://\")` — exact, case-sensitive, not whitespace-\ntolerant, so we treat more strings as URIs than it does), and that the\nlast-`@` userinfo rule is libpq''s (it uses the first). Both divergences\nstrip more than libpq reads, never less; that is now written down as the\ndirection, rather than claimed as equivalence.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01AkXu9aFDRDrMdbT3XJUdnc","tree":{"sha":"8f74b64f604d164d0f7acab829edf765c8dad5e6","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/healthkey-ai/exact/git/trees/8f74b64f604d164d0f7acab829edf765c8dad5e6"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/healthkey-ai/exact/git/commits/a8f8525f1bc5cc4fb74b516b08922e668152d1c0","verification":{"payload":"tree 8f74b64f604d164d0f7acab829edf765c8dad5e6\nparent e4253826b4ff3e8be5093668c443aead0eb44c13\nauthor LeoMo42 <69346755+LeoMo42@users.noreply.github.com> 1789486142 +0300\ncommitter LeoMo42 <69346755+LeoMo42@users.noreply.github.com> 1789486142 +0300\n\nfix(security): the refusals that were not credentials, and the tests a stub passed\n\nA differential fuzzer over 11,155 inputs, with a C oracle linked against\nlibpq 17 deciding what actually carries a credential, found no way to get\na password back out of either half. The remaining defects are the other\ndirection — refusing DSNs that carry nothing, disagreeing about which\nthose are, and tests that could not tell.\n\n**Refusals of things that are not credentials.** An empty `password=` is\nnot a password; libpq parses none out of it and the Python twin says so in\nas many words. bash refused it — and a refusal under `set -euo pipefail`\naborts container start, on the realistic `?password=${SECRET}` with SECRET\nunset. Likewise `host=h options=''-c ?password=x''`: the positional rule had\nbeen applied to the authority guard and not to the two query guards, so a\n`?` inside a conninfo *value* was read as a query separator. And Python\nrefused `postgresql://u@h/dbname password=s3cr3t`, where libpq reads the\nwhole tail as the database name — its keyword scans now run for keyword\nconninfo only, which is what they are for.\n\n**A password that decoded correctly and was then truncated.** Command\nsubstitution eats trailing newlines, so `%0A` reached PGPASSWORD without\nit: the connection authenticates with a different password than libpq\nwould have used. A sentinel byte preserves it.\n\n**One more twin disagreement.** `?password%20=s3cr3t` is not a parameter\nlibpq accepts, but the secret is in argv either way — the standard this\nmodule already applies to `PassWord=`. bash refused it; Python did not.\n\n**And the tests.** Replacing psql_dsn.sh with a stub that returns an empty\nstring for every input passed every case in the twin-agreement class: it\nasserted the secret was absent, never that the DSN survived. It now\nasserts what came back, which no stub satisfies — 23 failures against that\nstub, from zero. The helper also read only the first line of the bash\noutput, so a DSN containing a newline hid the rest; the corpus had stopped\none whitespace character short of the input that exploits it, which is\nexactly the shape of the last two rounds'' bugs. Both fixed, and the corpus\nnow carries the newline case.\n\nThree comments were saying things that are not true: that the init scripts\nrefuse an unrecognised scheme (they refuse a credential, whatever the\nscheme), that the positional rule mirrors libpq (libpq''s test is\n`strncmp(\"postgresql://\")` — exact, case-sensitive, not whitespace-\ntolerant, so we treat more strings as URIs than it does), and that the\nlast-`@` userinfo rule is libpq''s (it uses the first). Both divergences\nstrip more than libpq reads, never less; that is now written down as the\ndirection, rather than claimed as equivalence.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01AkXu9aFDRDrMdbT3XJUdnc\n","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN SSH SIGNATURE-----\nU1NIU0lHAAAAAQAAADMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAAAg65RgOXaobIWmShxVbQxxdrUES+\nprdck9i5CvM2zvHV4AAAADZ2l0AAAAAAAAAAZzaGE1MTIAAABTAAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5\nAAAAQLt40euAXmNQGtOU/c2OZ0AYirUflIBSMmsPe3IKg/WXqrx3EV1O0ozVYiCfUnefVV\n75uA+D9KALbk9PcMZg/Qw=\n-----END SSH SIGNATURE-----","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-15T19:09:30Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/69346755?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/LeoMo42","id":69346755,"login":"LeoMo42","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjY5MzQ2NzU1","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/healthkey-ai/exact/commit/a8f8525f1bc5cc4fb74b516b08922e668152d1c0","node_id":"C_kwDORYlqjNoAKGE4Zjg1MjVmMWJjNWNjNGZiNzRiNTE2YjA4OTIyZTY2ODE1MmQxYzA","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/healthkey-ai/exact/commit/e4253826b4ff3e8be5093668c443aead0eb44c13","sha":"e4253826b4ff3e8be5093668c443aead0eb44c13","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/healthkey-ai/exact/commits/e4253826b4ff3e8be5093668c443aead0eb44c13"}],"sha":"a8f8525f1bc5cc4fb74b516b08922e668152d1c0","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/healthkey-ai/exact/commits/a8f8525f1bc5cc4fb74b516b08922e668152d1c0"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/69346755?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/LeoMo42","id":69346755,"login":"LeoMo42","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjY5MzQ2NzU1","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/healthkey-ai/exact/commits/e4253826b4ff3e8be5093668c443aead0eb44c13/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-15T14:56:34Z","email":"69346755+LeoMo42@users.noreply.github.com","name":"LeoMo42"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-15T14:56:34Z","email":"69346755+LeoMo42@users.noreply.github.com","name":"LeoMo42"},"message":"fix(security): the bash twin skipped its own guards, and mine skipped a space\n\nRound five found two P0s, both mine, both from narrowing a classifier\nwithout asking what the narrowing excluded.\n\n**One leading space leaked the credential.** Python lstrips before\nmatching the scheme; the anchored bash pattern I added did not. So\n`  postgresql://u:s3cr3t@h/db` took the keyword-conninfo branch, whose\nonly test looks for a `password=` token that a URI userinfo does not\ncontain — and that branch returned before the end guards, which were\ninside the URI branch. Returned untouched, reported clean, into argv. The\nreviewer drove it through the real container path with real psql: the\npassword reached the process table AND the container log, because the\nprobe''s error handler prints the DSN psql rejected. Same call exists for\nthe patients DB.\n\n**And the guards only guarded one branch.** Narrowing the classifier\nmoved the fail-open rather than closing it: `my_scheme://u:pw@h/db` and\n`://u:pw@h/db` were declined by the rewrite and then examined by nothing.\nThe guards now run on the value about to be returned, whatever branch\nproduced it — which is what the Python side got two commits ago and bash\ndid not.\n\nFixing that the obvious way then broke the other direction: an\nunconditional authority scan reads `host=h options=''-c a://u:p@h''` as an\nauthority and refuses a DSN carrying no credential at all, which aborts\ncontainer start. Both halves now use libpq''s own positional rule — a URI\nis what *starts* with a scheme, so a `://` after a `=` is inside a value.\n`_looks_like_uri` in Python, the same test in bash.\n\nTwo more from the same pass: `_KEYWORD_PASSWORD` had no IGNORECASE and no\n`sslpassword`, so `host=h PassWord=s3cr3t` was returned clean — libpq\nwould reject the spelling, but argv exposure on a connection libpq refuses\nis the harm this module exists to prevent. And the \"#403\" comment in both\ninit scripts still claimed a refusal that had stopped happening.\n\nThe tests that let all this through were the point: they asserted what\neach half does, never that the two agree. There is now a corpus — every\nshape they drifted on, plus the ones that must keep working — checked\nagainst both, on the only invariant statable across a stripper and a\nrefuser: what comes back carries no credential, and a clean DSN is never\nrefused. Eight of its cases fail on the previous commit.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01AkXu9aFDRDrMdbT3XJUdnc","tree":{"sha":"57438167d4e3c639ac70bb2092fa5862e624f89c","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/healthkey-ai/exact/git/trees/57438167d4e3c639ac70bb2092fa5862e624f89c"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/healthkey-ai/exact/git/commits/e4253826b4ff3e8be5093668c443aead0eb44c13","verification":{"payload":"tree 57438167d4e3c639ac70bb2092fa5862e624f89c\nparent 246af61433e6e89855ad8006893badc1cb1136aa\nauthor LeoMo42 <69346755+LeoMo42@users.noreply.github.com> 1789484194 +0300\ncommitter LeoMo42 <69346755+LeoMo42@users.noreply.github.com> 1789484194 +0300\n\nfix(security): the bash twin skipped its own guards, and mine skipped a space\n\nRound five found two P0s, both mine, both from narrowing a classifier\nwithout asking what the narrowing excluded.\n\n**One leading space leaked the credential.** Python lstrips before\nmatching the scheme; the anchored bash pattern I added did not. So\n`  postgresql://u:s3cr3t@h/db` took the keyword-conninfo branch, whose\nonly test looks for a `password=` token that a URI userinfo does not\ncontain — and that branch returned before the end guards, which were\ninside the URI branch. Returned untouched, reported clean, into argv. The\nreviewer drove it through the real container path with real psql: the\npassword reached the process table AND the container log, because the\nprobe''s error handler prints the DSN psql rejected. Same call exists for\nthe patients DB.\n\n**And the guards only guarded one branch.** Narrowing the classifier\nmoved the fail-open rather than closing it: `my_scheme://u:pw@h/db` and\n`://u:pw@h/db` were declined by the rewrite and then examined by nothing.\nThe guards now run on the value about to be returned, whatever branch\nproduced it — which is what the Python side got two commits ago and bash\ndid not.\n\nFixing that the obvious way then broke the other direction: an\nunconditional authority scan reads `host=h options=''-c a://u:p@h''` as an\nauthority and refuses a DSN carrying no credential at all, which aborts\ncontainer start. Both halves now use libpq''s own positional rule — a URI\nis what *starts* with a scheme, so a `://` after a `=` is inside a value.\n`_looks_like_uri` in Python, the same test in bash.\n\nTwo more from the same pass: `_KEYWORD_PASSWORD` had no IGNORECASE and no\n`sslpassword`, so `host=h PassWord=s3cr3t` was returned clean — libpq\nwould reject the spelling, but argv exposure on a connection libpq refuses\nis the harm this module exists to prevent. And the \"#403\" comment in both\ninit scripts still claimed a refusal that had stopped happening.\n\nThe tests that let all this through were the point: they asserted what\neach half does, never that the two agree. There is now a corpus — every\nshape they drifted on, plus the ones that must keep working — checked\nagainst both, on the only invariant statable across a stripper and a\nrefuser: what comes back carries no credential, and a clean DSN is never\nrefused. Eight of its cases fail on the previous commit.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01AkXu9aFDRDrMdbT3XJUdnc\n","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN SSH SIGNATURE-----\nU1NIU0lHAAAAAQAAADMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAAAg65RgOXaobIWmShxVbQxxdrUES+\nprdck9i5CvM2zvHV4AAAADZ2l0AAAAAAAAAAZzaGE1MTIAAABTAAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5\nAAAAQN3zmrMTXUzEcwMlzIYsurt8Oydq1W++8DpcrgC2V4Ni7I4zfR2MfWLuurNb43xO95\nisnJzFxBodaY/7fuXcBwo=\n-----END SSH SIGNATURE-----","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-15T19:09:30Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/69346755?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/LeoMo42","id":69346755,"login":"LeoMo42","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjY5MzQ2NzU1","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/healthkey-ai/exact/commit/e4253826b4ff3e8be5093668c443aead0eb44c13","node_id":"C_kwDORYlqjNoAKGU0MjUzODI2YjRmZjNlOGJlNTA5MzY2OGM0NDNhZWFkMGViNDRjMTM","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/healthkey-ai/exact/commit/246af61433e6e89855ad8006893badc1cb1136aa","sha":"246af61433e6e89855ad8006893badc1cb1136aa","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/healthkey-ai/exact/commits/246af61433e6e89855ad8006893badc1cb1136aa"}],"sha":"e4253826b4ff3e8be5093668c443aead0eb44c13","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/healthkey-ai/exact/commits/e4253826b4ff3e8be5093668c443aead0eb44c13"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/69346755?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/LeoMo42","id":69346755,"login":"LeoMo42","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjY5MzQ2NzU1","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/healthkey-ai/exact/commits/246af61433e6e89855ad8006893badc1cb1136aa/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-15T14:40:40Z","email":"69346755+LeoMo42@users.noreply.github.com","name":"LeoMo42"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-15T14:40:40Z","email":"69346755+LeoMo42@users.noreply.github.com","name":"LeoMo42"},"message":"fix(security): an SSL key passphrase is not the database password\n\nRound four, both in the #403 helper as it arrived, not in my changes to it.\n\n**`sslpassword` was handed back as `PGPASSWORD`.** It decrypts the client\nkey; it is not the database user''s credential. So a certificate-\nauthenticated DSN lost its passphrase *and* authenticated with it — broken\ntwice, silently, by the fix meant to leave it working. And it cannot be\nrelocated: checked against libpq 17, the environment it reads offers\nPGPASSWORD and fifteen PGSSL* names, none of them PGSSLPASSWORD. With\nnowhere to move it, the only honest answer is the one this module already\nuses for what it cannot clean — refuse, and say why. An existing test\npinned the old behaviour; it is replaced rather than deleted, with the\nlibpq check written down so the next reader does not have to redo it.\n\n**A repeated `password=` used the wrong one.** libpq applies a keyword''s\nlater value, so `?password=old&password=new` connects as `new`. The loop\nkept the first and stripped both, authenticating with the value libpq\nwould have discarded — the same class as the userinfo-vs-query precedence\nfixed two commits ago, one level down.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01AkXu9aFDRDrMdbT3XJUdnc","tree":{"sha":"ab964c269d701545de96d60bae55bde93c966519","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/healthkey-ai/exact/git/trees/ab964c269d701545de96d60bae55bde93c966519"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/healthkey-ai/exact/git/commits/246af61433e6e89855ad8006893badc1cb1136aa","verification":{"payload":"tree ab964c269d701545de96d60bae55bde93c966519\nparent 2014770873804484d915bb9ce6e97432f6cd272a\nauthor LeoMo42 <69346755+LeoMo42@users.noreply.github.com> 1789483240 +0300\ncommitter LeoMo42 <69346755+LeoMo42@users.noreply.github.com> 1789483240 +0300\n\nfix(security): an SSL key passphrase is not the database password\n\nRound four, both in the #403 helper as it arrived, not in my changes to it.\n\n**`sslpassword` was handed back as `PGPASSWORD`.** It decrypts the client\nkey; it is not the database user''s credential. So a certificate-\nauthenticated DSN lost its passphrase *and* authenticated with it — broken\ntwice, silently, by the fix meant to leave it working. And it cannot be\nrelocated: checked against libpq 17, the environment it reads offers\nPGPASSWORD and fifteen PGSSL* names, none of them PGSSLPASSWORD. With\nnowhere to move it, the only honest answer is the one this module already\nuses for what it cannot clean — refuse, and say why. An existing test\npinned the old behaviour; it is replaced rather than deleted, with the\nlibpq check written down so the next reader does not have to redo it.\n\n**A repeated `password=` used the wrong one.** libpq applies a keyword''s\nlater value, so `?password=old&password=new` connects as `new`. The loop\nkept the first and stripped both, authenticating with the value libpq\nwould have discarded — the same class as the userinfo-vs-query precedence\nfixed two commits ago, one level down.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01AkXu9aFDRDrMdbT3XJUdnc\n","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN SSH SIGNATURE-----\nU1NIU0lHAAAAAQAAADMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAAAg65RgOXaobIWmShxVbQxxdrUES+\nprdck9i5CvM2zvHV4AAAADZ2l0AAAAAAAAAAZzaGE1MTIAAABTAAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5\nAAAAQCqs0PB0WWzu8FXcqKrXEt/wlDPTuTD+h/fnCuFHNW0b/t2KCVCQmMrZf7VvOB5O9f\n8uVT6OlQZvAFeZEwiwXwM=\n-----END SSH SIGNATURE-----","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-15T19:09:30Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/69346755?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/LeoMo42","id":69346755,"login":"LeoMo42","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjY5MzQ2NzU1","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/healthkey-ai/exact/commit/246af61433e6e89855ad8006893badc1cb1136aa","node_id":"C_kwDORYlqjNoAKDI0NmFmNjE0MzNlNmU4OTg1NWFkODAwNjg5M2JhZGMxY2IxMTM2YWE","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/healthkey-ai/exact/commit/2014770873804484d915bb9ce6e97432f6cd272a","sha":"2014770873804484d915bb9ce6e97432f6cd272a","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/healthkey-ai/exact/commits/2014770873804484d915bb9ce6e97432f6cd272a"}],"sha":"246af61433e6e89855ad8006893badc1cb1136aa","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/healthkey-ai/exact/commits/246af61433e6e89855ad8006893badc1cb1136aa"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/69346755?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/LeoMo42","id":69346755,"login":"LeoMo42","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjY5MzQ2NzU1","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/healthkey-ai/exact/commits/2014770873804484d915bb9ce6e97432f6cd272a/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-15T14:35:56Z","email":"69346755+LeoMo42@users.noreply.github.com","name":"LeoMo42"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-15T14:35:56Z","email":"69346755+LeoMo42@users.noreply.github.com","name":"LeoMo42"},"message":"fix(security): my own widening of the URI test made the shell fail open\n\nThe second review pass caught a regression I introduced one commit ago.\nWidening the shell''s scheme test to `*://*` — a glob, unanchored — meant\n`host=h user=u password=p://w`, a keyword conninfo whose *value* happens\nto contain `://`, took the URI branch. `${dsn%%://*}` swallows the left\nhalf, no `@` is found, the DSN is rebuilt identical to the input, and both\nend guards miss it because neither pattern matches what came back. Return\n0, credential intact, into every psql argv the init scripts run.\nMeasured: before my commit that input was refused; after it, `rc=0` with\nthe password still in `PSQL_DSN`. Fail-open, in the function whose only\njob is to fail closed. The test is now anchored and matches the Python\ntwin''s `[a-z][a-z0-9+.-]*://` exactly.\n\nPython had no such hole, which is the point: the twins disagreed on a\ncredential-bearing input, and `TestTheShellTwinAgreesWithPython` did not\nnotice because the only conninfo-with-`://` case was Python-only. It has a\nshell half now, along with the `postgis://` case that the widening was for\nand that nothing tested at all — reverting `_is_uri` to its old two-scheme\nform left the whole suite green.\n\nAlso, the backstop was asking the same question as the code it backs.\n`_assert_no_password` ran its URI scan only when `_is_uri` said so, so a\nstring the rewrite declined to touch — `my_scheme://u:pw@h/db`, `://u:pw@h`\n— came back unchanged *and* was reported clean. It now scans anything\ncontaining `://`, and checks the keyword form for every shape rather than\nonly for non-URIs.\n\nTwo loose ends from the same pass: docs/setup.md still documented the\nintrospection cap as 30 after it moved to 300, and nothing pinned the new\ndefault, so a silent revert was invisible. And the \"#403\" comment in\ninit_trials_db.sh stayed behind when the code it describes moved below the\nfeature gate.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01AkXu9aFDRDrMdbT3XJUdnc","tree":{"sha":"1b998556f483a01e23024360cb4df2a73a198fc1","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/healthkey-ai/exact/git/trees/1b998556f483a01e23024360cb4df2a73a198fc1"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/healthkey-ai/exact/git/commits/2014770873804484d915bb9ce6e97432f6cd272a","verification":{"payload":"tree 1b998556f483a01e23024360cb4df2a73a198fc1\nparent 08ca1a463322487708b67db9f9975c54ccf0ff43\nauthor LeoMo42 <69346755+LeoMo42@users.noreply.github.com> 1789482956 +0300\ncommitter LeoMo42 <69346755+LeoMo42@users.noreply.github.com> 1789482956 +0300\n\nfix(security): my own widening of the URI test made the shell fail open\n\nThe second review pass caught a regression I introduced one commit ago.\nWidening the shell''s scheme test to `*://*` — a glob, unanchored — meant\n`host=h user=u password=p://w`, a keyword conninfo whose *value* happens\nto contain `://`, took the URI branch. `${dsn%%://*}` swallows the left\nhalf, no `@` is found, the DSN is rebuilt identical to the input, and both\nend guards miss it because neither pattern matches what came back. Return\n0, credential intact, into every psql argv the init scripts run.\nMeasured: before my commit that input was refused; after it, `rc=0` with\nthe password still in `PSQL_DSN`. Fail-open, in the function whose only\njob is to fail closed. The test is now anchored and matches the Python\ntwin''s `[a-z][a-z0-9+.-]*://` exactly.\n\nPython had no such hole, which is the point: the twins disagreed on a\ncredential-bearing input, and `TestTheShellTwinAgreesWithPython` did not\nnotice because the only conninfo-with-`://` case was Python-only. It has a\nshell half now, along with the `postgis://` case that the widening was for\nand that nothing tested at all — reverting `_is_uri` to its old two-scheme\nform left the whole suite green.\n\nAlso, the backstop was asking the same question as the code it backs.\n`_assert_no_password` ran its URI scan only when `_is_uri` said so, so a\nstring the rewrite declined to touch — `my_scheme://u:pw@h/db`, `://u:pw@h`\n— came back unchanged *and* was reported clean. It now scans anything\ncontaining `://`, and checks the keyword form for every shape rather than\nonly for non-URIs.\n\nTwo loose ends from the same pass: docs/setup.md still documented the\nintrospection cap as 30 after it moved to 300, and nothing pinned the new\ndefault, so a silent revert was invisible. And the \"#403\" comment in\ninit_trials_db.sh stayed behind when the code it describes moved below the\nfeature gate.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01AkXu9aFDRDrMdbT3XJUdnc\n","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN SSH SIGNATURE-----\nU1NIU0lHAAAAAQAAADMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAAAg65RgOXaobIWmShxVbQxxdrUES+\nprdck9i5CvM2zvHV4AAAADZ2l0AAAAAAAAAAZzaGE1MTIAAABTAAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5\nAAAAQCnfG+D27v29gmeMbXOovJ3XLbuFukUSAKai292QJSEDhAy1cNKv8eNiZBPGlL24w9\nEiWfrThn3dUBjhTKw64QQ=\n-----END SSH SIGNATURE-----","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-15T19:09:30Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/69346755?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/LeoMo42","id":69346755,"login":"LeoMo42","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjY5MzQ2NzU1","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/healthkey-ai/exact/commit/2014770873804484d915bb9ce6e97432f6cd272a","node_id":"C_kwDORYlqjNoAKDIwMTQ3NzA4NzM4MDQ0ODRkOTE1YmI5Y2U2ZTk3NDMyZjZjZDI3MmE","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/healthkey-ai/exact/commit/08ca1a463322487708b67db9f9975c54ccf0ff43","sha":"08ca1a463322487708b67db9f9975c54ccf0ff43","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/healthkey-ai/exact/commits/08ca1a463322487708b67db9f9975c54ccf0ff43"}],"sha":"2014770873804484d915bb9ce6e97432f6cd272a","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/healthkey-ai/exact/commits/2014770873804484d915bb9ce6e97432f6cd272a"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/69346755?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/LeoMo42","id":69346755,"login":"LeoMo42","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjY5MzQ2NzU1","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/healthkey-ai/exact/commits/08ca1a463322487708b67db9f9975c54ccf0ff43/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-15T14:24:34Z","email":"69346755+LeoMo42@users.noreply.github.com","name":"LeoMo42"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-15T14:24:34Z","email":"69346755+LeoMo42@users.noreply.github.com","name":"LeoMo42"},"message":"fix(security): the twins disagreed about which DSNs carry a password\n\nRunning the review over the final state — the tail of this branch had only\nbeen seen by one of the two — turned up two more, both in the #403 helper.\n\n**The shell twin let spellings through that Python strips.** It matched the\nliteral lowercase `password=`, so `?Password=` and `?%70assword=` stayed in\n`PSQL_DSN` and went into every psql argv the init scripts run: the exact\nexposure the function exists to refuse. libpq percent-decodes parameter\nnames, which is why the Python side decodes the key before matching — and\nwhy a literal match here also left the two halves disagreeing about the\nsame input. Decoding in bash is the part worth not attempting (`printf\n''%b''` mangles backslashes), and this function refuses rather than strips,\nso it can be blunt: case-insensitive on the name, plus any `%` in a\nparameter name is itself grounds to refuse. That covers every encoding\nwithout decoding anything.\n\n**Python authenticated with the password libpq would have discarded.**\nGiven both `postgresql://u:old@h/db?password=new`, libpq parses the\nuserinfo first and then the query, and a later setting of a keyword\nreplaces the earlier one — it connects as `new`. The helper stripped both\nand kept `old`, so a DSN that worked before the fix fails authentication\nafter it. The query password now wins, which is what the connection would\nhave done.\n\nBoth are pinned, including that the blunt shell rule still accepts an\nordinary `?sslmode=require` DSN — a false refusal there aborts container\nstart, which is worse than what it guards against.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01AkXu9aFDRDrMdbT3XJUdnc","tree":{"sha":"ea7fb061604bff3fe01bb47da30836a23eba964d","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/healthkey-ai/exact/git/trees/ea7fb061604bff3fe01bb47da30836a23eba964d"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/healthkey-ai/exact/git/commits/08ca1a463322487708b67db9f9975c54ccf0ff43","verification":{"payload":"tree ea7fb061604bff3fe01bb47da30836a23eba964d\nparent 86f5a7446f1f783c234a5dddffda9d87b4081061\nauthor LeoMo42 <69346755+LeoMo42@users.noreply.github.com> 1789482274 +0300\ncommitter LeoMo42 <69346755+LeoMo42@users.noreply.github.com> 1789482274 +0300\n\nfix(security): the twins disagreed about which DSNs carry a password\n\nRunning the review over the final state — the tail of this branch had only\nbeen seen by one of the two — turned up two more, both in the #403 helper.\n\n**The shell twin let spellings through that Python strips.** It matched the\nliteral lowercase `password=`, so `?Password=` and `?%70assword=` stayed in\n`PSQL_DSN` and went into every psql argv the init scripts run: the exact\nexposure the function exists to refuse. libpq percent-decodes parameter\nnames, which is why the Python side decodes the key before matching — and\nwhy a literal match here also left the two halves disagreeing about the\nsame input. Decoding in bash is the part worth not attempting (`printf\n''%b''` mangles backslashes), and this function refuses rather than strips,\nso it can be blunt: case-insensitive on the name, plus any `%` in a\nparameter name is itself grounds to refuse. That covers every encoding\nwithout decoding anything.\n\n**Python authenticated with the password libpq would have discarded.**\nGiven both `postgresql://u:old@h/db?password=new`, libpq parses the\nuserinfo first and then the query, and a later setting of a keyword\nreplaces the earlier one — it connects as `new`. The helper stripped both\nand kept `old`, so a DSN that worked before the fix fails authentication\nafter it. The query password now wins, which is what the connection would\nhave done.\n\nBoth are pinned, including that the blunt shell rule still accepts an\nordinary `?sslmode=require` DSN — a false refusal there aborts container\nstart, which is worse than what it guards against.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01AkXu9aFDRDrMdbT3XJUdnc\n","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN SSH SIGNATURE-----\nU1NIU0lHAAAAAQAAADMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAAAg65RgOXaobIWmShxVbQxxdrUES+\nprdck9i5CvM2zvHV4AAAADZ2l0AAAAAAAAAAZzaGE1MTIAAABTAAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5\nAAAAQMUg+1FBNJ9mFsCv5+Et7kkfP633QntsnHqj55Cl7YMQ6bfTUMh9ynQqJHexAGM4tP\nKlbdOWJjNabomhWpd+FAM=\n-----END SSH SIGNATURE-----","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-15T19:09:30Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/69346755?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/LeoMo42","id":69346755,"login":"LeoMo42","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjY5MzQ2NzU1","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/healthkey-ai/exact/commit/08ca1a463322487708b67db9f9975c54ccf0ff43","node_id":"C_kwDORYlqjNoAKDA4Y2ExYTQ2MzMyMjQ4NzcwOGI2N2RiOWY5OTc1YzU0Y2NmMGZmNDM","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/healthkey-ai/exact/commit/86f5a7446f1f783c234a5dddffda9d87b4081061","sha":"86f5a7446f1f783c234a5dddffda9d87b4081061","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/healthkey-ai/exact/commits/86f5a7446f1f783c234a5dddffda9d87b4081061"}],"sha":"08ca1a463322487708b67db9f9975c54ccf0ff43","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/healthkey-ai/exact/commits/08ca1a463322487708b67db9f9975c54ccf0ff43"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/69346755?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/LeoMo42","id":69346755,"login":"LeoMo42","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjY5MzQ2NzU1","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/healthkey-ai/exact/commits/86f5a7446f1f783c234a5dddffda9d87b4081061/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-15T13:15:21Z","email":"69346755+LeoMo42@users.noreply.github.com","name":"LeoMo42"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-15T13:16:24Z","email":"69346755+LeoMo42@users.noreply.github.com","name":"LeoMo42"},"message":"fix(auth): raise the introspection allowance the cache removal emptied\n\nBoth reviewers came back to the same place, so: #404 deleted the cache\nthat made this budget a per-sign-in one, and nothing resized it. It now\ncounts requests — a page firing several API calls spends several — so\n30/60s empties under ordinary use and every introspection caller 401s\nuntil the window rolls. Only DEBUG/local is affected, the path being off\nelsewhere, but that is the local stack and the e2e harness.\n\nI first tried refunding a confirmed-`active` answer, so the ceiling would\nonly count calls the portal did not vouch for. Four of the existing tests\nfailed, and they were right to: the class caps *outbound calls*, and a\ncaller holding one valid token can occupy a worker in a 5s POST exactly\nlike a caller holding none. The exemption would have opened the hole the\nceiling exists to close. Reverted; the allowance is raised instead, which\nis the lever the author left as an env var.\n\n300/60s is five per second per process — still a bound on what this\nservice can be made to send at the portal, and clear of ordinary use.\nThe reasoning is recorded next to the number and in the class docstring,\nwhich until now still explained a sizing that no longer applied.\n\nAlso removing four generated files (`frontend/vite.config.js`,\n`vite.remote.config.js`, and their `.d.ts`) that a `git add -A` of mine\nswept up after a type-check run in this worktree. They are build output,\nand `vite.config.js` is the worse kind: vite prefers it over the `.ts`, so\na stale committed copy silently becomes the config `npm run dev` uses\nwhile edits to the maintained source are ignored. Added to .gitignore so\nthe next type-check does not do it again.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01AkXu9aFDRDrMdbT3XJUdnc","tree":{"sha":"f4393f94a605c3148bd8736a27236df49598dda5","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/healthkey-ai/exact/git/trees/f4393f94a605c3148bd8736a27236df49598dda5"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/healthkey-ai/exact/git/commits/86f5a7446f1f783c234a5dddffda9d87b4081061","verification":{"payload":"tree f4393f94a605c3148bd8736a27236df49598dda5\nparent 30e67f5096804919b836e00269f2b8a0ed8f7ae4\nauthor LeoMo42 <69346755+LeoMo42@users.noreply.github.com> 1789478121 +0300\ncommitter LeoMo42 <69346755+LeoMo42@users.noreply.github.com> 1789478184 +0300\n\nfix(auth): raise the introspection allowance the cache removal emptied\n\nBoth reviewers came back to the same place, so: #404 deleted the cache\nthat made this budget a per-sign-in one, and nothing resized it. It now\ncounts requests — a page firing several API calls spends several — so\n30/60s empties under ordinary use and every introspection caller 401s\nuntil the window rolls. Only DEBUG/local is affected, the path being off\nelsewhere, but that is the local stack and the e2e harness.\n\nI first tried refunding a confirmed-`active` answer, so the ceiling would\nonly count calls the portal did not vouch for. Four of the existing tests\nfailed, and they were right to: the class caps *outbound calls*, and a\ncaller holding one valid token can occupy a worker in a 5s POST exactly\nlike a caller holding none. The exemption would have opened the hole the\nceiling exists to close. Reverted; the allowance is raised instead, which\nis the lever the author left as an env var.\n\n300/60s is five per second per process — still a bound on what this\nservice can be made to send at the portal, and clear of ordinary use.\nThe reasoning is recorded next to the number and in the class docstring,\nwhich until now still explained a sizing that no longer applied.\n\nAlso removing four generated files (`frontend/vite.config.js`,\n`vite.remote.config.js`, and their `.d.ts`) that a `git add -A` of mine\nswept up after a type-check run in this worktree. They are build output,\nand `vite.config.js` is the worse kind: vite prefers it over the `.ts`, so\na stale committed copy silently becomes the config `npm run dev` uses\nwhile edits to the maintained source are ignored. Added to .gitignore so\nthe next type-check does not do it again.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01AkXu9aFDRDrMdbT3XJUdnc\n","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN SSH SIGNATURE-----\nU1NIU0lHAAAAAQAAADMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAAAg65RgOXaobIWmShxVbQxxdrUES+\nprdck9i5CvM2zvHV4AAAADZ2l0AAAAAAAAAAZzaGE1MTIAAABTAAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5\nAAAAQKlUV9tIl/qn3dBInLx3ISzKqhFEuvte7Fjsm2zfYJf2c/Jz68/igWdqmJpS5UvB8Z\n2JucmO6A3yZ7vtj/kUYw8=\n-----END SSH SIGNATURE-----","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-15T13:16:56Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/69346755?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/LeoMo42","id":69346755,"login":"LeoMo42","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjY5MzQ2NzU1","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/healthkey-ai/exact/commit/86f5a7446f1f783c234a5dddffda9d87b4081061","node_id":"C_kwDORYlqjNoAKDg2ZjVhNzQ0NmYxZjc4M2MyMzRhNWRkZGZmZGE5ZDg3YjQwODEwNjE","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/healthkey-ai/exact/commit/30e67f5096804919b836e00269f2b8a0ed8f7ae4","sha":"30e67f5096804919b836e00269f2b8a0ed8f7ae4","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/healthkey-ai/exact/commits/30e67f5096804919b836e00269f2b8a0ed8f7ae4"}],"sha":"86f5a7446f1f783c234a5dddffda9d87b4081061","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/healthkey-ai/exact/commits/86f5a7446f1f783c234a5dddffda9d87b4081061"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/69346755?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/LeoMo42","id":69346755,"login":"LeoMo42","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjY5MzQ2NzU1","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/healthkey-ai/exact/commits/30e67f5096804919b836e00269f2b8a0ed8f7ae4/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-15T13:08:06Z","email":"69346755+LeoMo42@users.noreply.github.com","name":"LeoMo42"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-15T13:08:06Z","email":"69346755+LeoMo42@users.noreply.github.com","name":"LeoMo42"},"message":"fix(security): a non-libpq URI scheme walked past the credential guard\n\nFresh-context review of the port found three more things, two of them in\nthe fixes being ported and so still open where they came from.\n\n**`postgis://` was not treated as a URI.** `_is_uri` matched only the two\nschemes libpq itself connects to, so every other scheme fell to the\nkeyword-conninfo branch — where a URI-shaped credential contains no\n`password=` token. The URL came back unchanged, password included, and\n`_assert_no_password` waved it through on the same test: reported clean,\nhanded to psql in argv. That is precisely the silent pass the module says\ncannot happen. It matters here rather than in theory: settings.py\ndocuments `postgis://` as the deploy convention for the shared Cloud SQL\ninstance, and dj_database_url accepts it. libpq then refuses to connect,\nso the leak is a failed command — with the credential in its\n/proc/<pid>/cmdline for as long as it lives. Anything with an authority is\na URI now, in both the Python and the shell twin.\n\n**#403''s refusal gated a path it has nothing to do with.**\ninit_trials_db.sh ran the DSN split before the\nTRIALS_DATABASE_INIT_FROM_BACKUP gate, so a credentialed DSN aborted the\nscript — and with `set -e` in the entrypoint, gunicorn never starts. On a\ndeployment that never asked for the restore. init_patients_db.sh already\nhad the right order; measured both ways: before, exit 1; after, the\nordinary \"not enabled — skipping\".\n\n**A budget was sized against a premise #404 removed.** The PHR\nintrospection cap reasoned that successful verifications were cached, so\nonly failures re-issued the 5s POST. With the cache gone it bounds\nrequests, not sign-ins, and one page that fires several API calls spends\nseveral of them — 30/60s empties under ordinary use and every\nintrospection caller gets a 401 until the window rolls. Deployments are\nunaffected (the path is off outside DEBUG/local), so this corrects the\nreasoning where it is written rather than changing the number, which\nbelongs to whoever runs that stack.\n\nAlso: the dependency bump from the same #397 was left behind — dev is ten\nsecurity releases behind on the Django LTS line. Picked, and the suite run\nagainst the actual new versions rather than the ones already installed\nhere: 1168 passed on Django 5.2.17 / requests 2.33.1. Plus two stale\ndocstrings that still recommended passing a credential on a command line.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01AkXu9aFDRDrMdbT3XJUdnc","tree":{"sha":"45165cc68f3d43ad9da2e1c0da9e53cb5f7e8d80","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/healthkey-ai/exact/git/trees/45165cc68f3d43ad9da2e1c0da9e53cb5f7e8d80"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/healthkey-ai/exact/git/commits/30e67f5096804919b836e00269f2b8a0ed8f7ae4","verification":{"payload":"tree 45165cc68f3d43ad9da2e1c0da9e53cb5f7e8d80\nparent d45d90e2024a91cb5d0566f2eda9b717d71771bd\nauthor LeoMo42 <69346755+LeoMo42@users.noreply.github.com> 1789477686 +0300\ncommitter LeoMo42 <69346755+LeoMo42@users.noreply.github.com> 1789477686 +0300\n\nfix(security): a non-libpq URI scheme walked past the credential guard\n\nFresh-context review of the port found three more things, two of them in\nthe fixes being ported and so still open where they came from.\n\n**`postgis://` was not treated as a URI.** `_is_uri` matched only the two\nschemes libpq itself connects to, so every other scheme fell to the\nkeyword-conninfo branch — where a URI-shaped credential contains no\n`password=` token. The URL came back unchanged, password included, and\n`_assert_no_password` waved it through on the same test: reported clean,\nhanded to psql in argv. That is precisely the silent pass the module says\ncannot happen. It matters here rather than in theory: settings.py\ndocuments `postgis://` as the deploy convention for the shared Cloud SQL\ninstance, and dj_database_url accepts it. libpq then refuses to connect,\nso the leak is a failed command — with the credential in its\n/proc/<pid>/cmdline for as long as it lives. Anything with an authority is\na URI now, in both the Python and the shell twin.\n\n**#403''s refusal gated a path it has nothing to do with.**\ninit_trials_db.sh ran the DSN split before the\nTRIALS_DATABASE_INIT_FROM_BACKUP gate, so a credentialed DSN aborted the\nscript — and with `set -e` in the entrypoint, gunicorn never starts. On a\ndeployment that never asked for the restore. init_patients_db.sh already\nhad the right order; measured both ways: before, exit 1; after, the\nordinary \"not enabled — skipping\".\n\n**A budget was sized against a premise #404 removed.** The PHR\nintrospection cap reasoned that successful verifications were cached, so\nonly failures re-issued the 5s POST. With the cache gone it bounds\nrequests, not sign-ins, and one page that fires several API calls spends\nseveral of them — 30/60s empties under ordinary use and every\nintrospection caller gets a 401 until the window rolls. Deployments are\nunaffected (the path is off outside DEBUG/local), so this corrects the\nreasoning where it is written rather than changing the number, which\nbelongs to whoever runs that stack.\n\nAlso: the dependency bump from the same #397 was left behind — dev is ten\nsecurity releases behind on the Django LTS line. Picked, and the suite run\nagainst the actual new versions rather than the ones already installed\nhere: 1168 passed on Django 5.2.17 / requests 2.33.1. Plus two stale\ndocstrings that still recommended passing a credential on a command line.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01AkXu9aFDRDrMdbT3XJUdnc\n","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN SSH SIGNATURE-----\nU1NIU0lHAAAAAQAAADMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAAAg65RgOXaobIWmShxVbQxxdrUES+\nprdck9i5CvM2zvHV4AAAADZ2l0AAAAAAAAAAZzaGE1MTIAAABTAAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5\nAAAAQJXI5ytsBmYbG2FJ0LmpOk2X5CJSYUwwtrLV0etjGmWXVry/Hk7jW2fmn2ljF0KtBG\nltNBzMDiL0biq0QbExVQI=\n-----END SSH SIGNATURE-----","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-15T13:16:56Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/69346755?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/LeoMo42","id":69346755,"login":"LeoMo42","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjY5MzQ2NzU1","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/healthkey-ai/exact/commit/30e67f5096804919b836e00269f2b8a0ed8f7ae4","node_id":"C_kwDORYlqjNoAKDMwZTY3ZjUwOTY4MDQ5MTliODM2ZTAwMjY5ZjJiOGEwZWQ4ZjdhZTQ","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/healthkey-ai/exact/commit/d45d90e2024a91cb5d0566f2eda9b717d71771bd","sha":"d45d90e2024a91cb5d0566f2eda9b717d71771bd","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/healthkey-ai/exact/commits/d45d90e2024a91cb5d0566f2eda9b717d71771bd"}],"sha":"30e67f5096804919b836e00269f2b8a0ed8f7ae4","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/healthkey-ai/exact/commits/30e67f5096804919b836e00269f2b8a0ed8f7ae4"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/2562446?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeonidMorozov/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeonidMorozov/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeonidMorozov/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeonidMorozov/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/LeonidMorozov","id":2562446,"login":"LeonidMorozov","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjI1NjI0NDY=","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeonidMorozov/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeonidMorozov/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeonidMorozov/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeonidMorozov/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeonidMorozov/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeonidMorozov","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/healthkey-ai/exact/commits/d45d90e2024a91cb5d0566f2eda9b717d71771bd/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-08-30T18:34:55Z","email":"lmmobi98@gmail.com","name":"Leonid Morozov"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-15T13:07:07Z","email":"69346755+LeoMo42@users.noreply.github.com","name":"LeoMo42"},"message":"chore(deps): Django 5.2.17, python-dotenv 1.2.3, requests 2.33.1 (#397)\n\n`pip-audit` reported 29 advisories against Django 5.2.7 — ten security releases\nbehind the head of the same LTS line — plus one each for python-dotenv and\nrequests. All three are patch bumps within their current minor, no API surface\nchanges.\n\nNo reachable exploit was found for any specific Django advisory in this\nconfiguration: `FilteredRelation` is unused, the service is WSGI rather than\nASGI, no cache middleware is installed, `RasterField`/`GDALRaster` are unused,\nGeoDjango only ever constructs `Point(float, float)` rather than parsing WKT\nfrom request data, `list_editable` is unused, and every `Q(**{...})` uses static\nkeys from `USER_TO_TRIAL_ATTRS_MAPPING` with no `_connector`. This is patch\ncurrency, not an incident.\n\n`requests` goes to 2.33.1 rather than 2.33.0 on review: 2.33.0 still carries the\n`_parse_content_type_header` bug where a `Content-Type` parameter with no `=`\nyields a bool, so `Response.encoding` becomes `True` and\n`iter_lines(decode_unicode=True)` raises `AttributeError`. That is a live path —\n`vocab_mirror/promop_vocab_client.py:172` streams the NDJSON snapshot that way,\nand the error escapes outside the `except requests.RequestException` guard at\n:178 instead of surfacing as a `VocabSyncError`. Confirmed on 2.33.1:\n`_parse_content_type_header(''application/x-ndjson; charset'')` now returns\n`(''application/x-ndjson'', {})`. 2.33.1 also tightens the header validators from\n`$` to `\\Z`, which matters because both promop clients build\n`Authorization: Bearer {token}` from a remote OAuth JSON body.\n\n`python-dotenv` goes to 1.2.3 for the same reason: 1.2.2 carries no fix we need,\nwhile 1.2.3 fixes a UTF-8-BOM `.env` that silently drops the first variable —\nthe more relevant bug for a dotenv-driven deploy.\n\nAfter the bump `pip-audit` reports one remaining advisory, `pytest` (dev-only,\nfixed in 9.0.3), left for a separate change since it touches the test toolchain.\n\nFull suite in a clean venv: 1381 passed, 5 skipped, 2 xfailed. Also verified\nunder pip 24.0 (what CI forces): `pip install -r requirements.txt` including the\neditable `matcher_app`, `makemigrations --check`, `manage.py check`,\n`collectstatic`, and `migrate --run-syncdb` against a real PostGIS database.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01PuGkd1Cs4w1uCDFYKdVwJZ\n(cherry picked from commit 5ec2d61197d8d779669310de79a25925c182d8ca)","tree":{"sha":"7ed74ee08efeecf15982362e7789a4faa8598bdd","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/healthkey-ai/exact/git/trees/7ed74ee08efeecf15982362e7789a4faa8598bdd"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/healthkey-ai/exact/git/commits/d45d90e2024a91cb5d0566f2eda9b717d71771bd","verification":{"payload":"tree 7ed74ee08efeecf15982362e7789a4faa8598bdd\nparent 0f4c314c1d78939a6c26186d43a40092efc2ab02\nauthor Leonid Morozov <lmmobi98@gmail.com> 1788114895 +0300\ncommitter LeoMo42 <69346755+LeoMo42@users.noreply.github.com> 1789477627 +0300\n\nchore(deps): Django 5.2.17, python-dotenv 1.2.3, requests 2.33.1 (#397)\n\n`pip-audit` reported 29 advisories against Django 5.2.7 — ten security releases\nbehind the head of the same LTS line — plus one each for python-dotenv and\nrequests. All three are patch bumps within their current minor, no API surface\nchanges.\n\nNo reachable exploit was found for any specific Django advisory in this\nconfiguration: `FilteredRelation` is unused, the service is WSGI rather than\nASGI, no cache middleware is installed, `RasterField`/`GDALRaster` are unused,\nGeoDjango only ever constructs `Point(float, float)` rather than parsing WKT\nfrom request data, `list_editable` is unused, and every `Q(**{...})` uses static\nkeys from `USER_TO_TRIAL_ATTRS_MAPPING` with no `_connector`. This is patch\ncurrency, not an incident.\n\n`requests` goes to 2.33.1 rather than 2.33.0 on review: 2.33.0 still carries the\n`_parse_content_type_header` bug where a `Content-Type` parameter with no `=`\nyields a bool, so `Response.encoding` becomes `True` and\n`iter_lines(decode_unicode=True)` raises `AttributeError`. That is a live path —\n`vocab_mirror/promop_vocab_client.py:172` streams the NDJSON snapshot that way,\nand the error escapes outside the `except requests.RequestException` guard at\n:178 instead of surfacing as a `VocabSyncError`. Confirmed on 2.33.1:\n`_parse_content_type_header(''application/x-ndjson; charset'')` now returns\n`(''application/x-ndjson'', {})`. 2.33.1 also tightens the header validators from\n`$` to `\\Z`, which matters because both promop clients build\n`Authorization: Bearer {token}` from a remote OAuth JSON body.\n\n`python-dotenv` goes to 1.2.3 for the same reason: 1.2.2 carries no fix we need,\nwhile 1.2.3 fixes a UTF-8-BOM `.env` that silently drops the first variable —\nthe more relevant bug for a dotenv-driven deploy.\n\nAfter the bump `pip-audit` reports one remaining advisory, `pytest` (dev-only,\nfixed in 9.0.3), left for a separate change since it touches the test toolchain.\n\nFull suite in a clean venv: 1381 passed, 5 skipped, 2 xfailed. Also verified\nunder pip 24.0 (what CI forces): `pip install -r requirements.txt` including the\neditable `matcher_app`, `makemigrations --check`, `manage.py check`,\n`collectstatic`, and `migrate --run-syncdb` against a real PostGIS database.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01PuGkd1Cs4w1uCDFYKdVwJZ\n(cherry picked from commit 5ec2d61197d8d779669310de79a25925c182d8ca)\n","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN SSH SIGNATURE-----\nU1NIU0lHAAAAAQAAADMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAAAg65RgOXaobIWmShxVbQxxdrUES+\nprdck9i5CvM2zvHV4AAAADZ2l0AAAAAAAAAAZzaGE1MTIAAABTAAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5\nAAAAQFxO2RJXFqS/pPY+qvUPgeVBgS6trZoohn47Hepoy/QStVBeM5+HGGgq1zWcMc0e1m\n5FNQriOehCRNMPkop/iAc=\n-----END SSH SIGNATURE-----","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-15T13:16:56Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/69346755?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/LeoMo42","id":69346755,"login":"LeoMo42","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjY5MzQ2NzU1","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/healthkey-ai/exact/commit/d45d90e2024a91cb5d0566f2eda9b717d71771bd","node_id":"C_kwDORYlqjNoAKGQ0NWQ5MGUyMDI0YTkxY2I1ZDA1NjZmMmVkYTliNzE3ZDcxNzcxYmQ","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/healthkey-ai/exact/commit/0f4c314c1d78939a6c26186d43a40092efc2ab02","sha":"0f4c314c1d78939a6c26186d43a40092efc2ab02","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/healthkey-ai/exact/commits/0f4c314c1d78939a6c26186d43a40092efc2ab02"}],"sha":"d45d90e2024a91cb5d0566f2eda9b717d71771bd","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/healthkey-ai/exact/commits/d45d90e2024a91cb5d0566f2eda9b717d71771bd"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/69346755?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/LeoMo42","id":69346755,"login":"LeoMo42","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjY5MzQ2NzU1","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/healthkey-ai/exact/commits/0f4c314c1d78939a6c26186d43a40092efc2ab02/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-15T12:53:59Z","email":"69346755+LeoMo42@users.noreply.github.com","name":"LeoMo42"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-15T12:53:59Z","email":"69346755+LeoMo42@users.noreply.github.com","name":"LeoMo42"},"message":"fix(security): two holes in the fixes being ported, found reviewing the port\n\nReview of the backport found both of these in the original fixes, so they\nare live on the branch they came from as well as here.\n\n**A pathless URI kept its password in argv.** `_split_authority` ended the\nauthority at the first `/`, and `postgresql://user@host?password=secret`\nhas none — so the query became part of the authority, the strip never saw\na tail, and neither did the `_assert_no_password` backstop that exists to\ncatch exactly this. The credential went to psql on the command line: the\nsingle outcome #403 was written to prevent, on the shape most likely to be\nwritten by hand. The query now terminates the authority too, but only\nafter the userinfo, because libpq reads a `?` before the `@` as part of\nthe password and truncating there would turn a working credential into an\nauthentication failure that looks like a bad password. The shell twin was\nalready safe — it refuses these outright — so only Python was exposed.\n\n**A failed probe still authorised DROP SCHEMA.** #397 made the init\nscripts'' probes fail closed on the principle that \"a failed probe is not\nevidence that the database is empty\", and left the third one — whether the\ntable resolves through search_path outside `public` — discarding its exit\nstatus and its stderr. A transient failure there yields an empty string,\nwhich reads as \"nothing outside public\" and falls through to\n`DROP SCHEMA public CASCADE` on a database nobody managed to inspect.\n\nDemonstrated rather than argued: with a psql stub that fails only that\nprobe, the old script walks past it to \"Backup source: …\", i.e. into the\nrestore path; the fixed one stops at \"refusing to restore\". That scenario\nis now a test that runs the real script against the stub — it needs bash 4\nfor the script''s own syntax, so it skips on macOS''s bash 3.2 and runs in\nCI.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01AkXu9aFDRDrMdbT3XJUdnc","tree":{"sha":"a1355095c1ddc40fe787c25d7431a1074d8bad13","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/healthkey-ai/exact/git/trees/a1355095c1ddc40fe787c25d7431a1074d8bad13"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/healthkey-ai/exact/git/commits/0f4c314c1d78939a6c26186d43a40092efc2ab02","verification":{"payload":"tree a1355095c1ddc40fe787c25d7431a1074d8bad13\nparent a4f60f7905e4cf3eeb4739e153aba06b450d6da2\nauthor LeoMo42 <69346755+LeoMo42@users.noreply.github.com> 1789476839 +0300\ncommitter LeoMo42 <69346755+LeoMo42@users.noreply.github.com> 1789476839 +0300\n\nfix(security): two holes in the fixes being ported, found reviewing the port\n\nReview of the backport found both of these in the original fixes, so they\nare live on the branch they came from as well as here.\n\n**A pathless URI kept its password in argv.** `_split_authority` ended the\nauthority at the first `/`, and `postgresql://user@host?password=secret`\nhas none — so the query became part of the authority, the strip never saw\na tail, and neither did the `_assert_no_password` backstop that exists to\ncatch exactly this. The credential went to psql on the command line: the\nsingle outcome #403 was written to prevent, on the shape most likely to be\nwritten by hand. The query now terminates the authority too, but only\nafter the userinfo, because libpq reads a `?` before the `@` as part of\nthe password and truncating there would turn a working credential into an\nauthentication failure that looks like a bad password. The shell twin was\nalready safe — it refuses these outright — so only Python was exposed.\n\n**A failed probe still authorised DROP SCHEMA.** #397 made the init\nscripts'' probes fail closed on the principle that \"a failed probe is not\nevidence that the database is empty\", and left the third one — whether the\ntable resolves through search_path outside `public` — discarding its exit\nstatus and its stderr. A transient failure there yields an empty string,\nwhich reads as \"nothing outside public\" and falls through to\n`DROP SCHEMA public CASCADE` on a database nobody managed to inspect.\n\nDemonstrated rather than argued: with a psql stub that fails only that\nprobe, the old script walks past it to \"Backup source: …\", i.e. into the\nrestore path; the fixed one stops at \"refusing to restore\". That scenario\nis now a test that runs the real script against the stub — it needs bash 4\nfor the script''s own syntax, so it skips on macOS''s bash 3.2 and runs in\nCI.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01AkXu9aFDRDrMdbT3XJUdnc\n","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN SSH SIGNATURE-----\nU1NIU0lHAAAAAQAAADMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAAAg65RgOXaobIWmShxVbQxxdrUES+\nprdck9i5CvM2zvHV4AAAADZ2l0AAAAAAAAAAZzaGE1MTIAAABTAAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5\nAAAAQE9iCkAZsPyL2CtO9qMVELhqjKYjvmMJUG2GyAkl2mIBIKLZk5dMZEUKWeXM611H98\no5SPKNI862dV3DqAXQ1Qc=\n-----END SSH SIGNATURE-----","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-15T13:01:39Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/69346755?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/LeoMo42","id":69346755,"login":"LeoMo42","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjY5MzQ2NzU1","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/healthkey-ai/exact/commit/0f4c314c1d78939a6c26186d43a40092efc2ab02","node_id":"C_kwDORYlqjNoAKDBmNGMzMTRjMWQ3ODkzOWE2YzI2MTg2ZDQzYTQwMDkyZWZjMmFiMDI","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/healthkey-ai/exact/commit/a4f60f7905e4cf3eeb4739e153aba06b450d6da2","sha":"a4f60f7905e4cf3eeb4739e153aba06b450d6da2","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/healthkey-ai/exact/commits/a4f60f7905e4cf3eeb4739e153aba06b450d6da2"}],"sha":"0f4c314c1d78939a6c26186d43a40092efc2ab02","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/healthkey-ai/exact/commits/0f4c314c1d78939a6c26186d43a40092efc2ab02"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/69346755?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/LeoMo42","id":69346755,"login":"LeoMo42","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjY5MzQ2NzU1","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/healthkey-ai/exact/commits/a4f60f7905e4cf3eeb4739e153aba06b450d6da2/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-08T13:15:03Z","email":"leonidmorozov@icloud.com","name":"Leonid Morozov"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-15T12:43:05Z","email":"69346755+LeoMo42@users.noreply.github.com","name":"LeoMo42"},"message":"fix(security): remove the verified-token cache, which never re-read the token (#404)\n\n`PartnerAuthentication` cached each verification for `AUTH_TOKEN_CACHE_TTL`\n(60s by default), keyed by `SHA256(token)[:32]`, and returned it without\nconsulting any provider:\n\n    cached = self._from_cache(token)\n    if cached is not None:\n        return cached\n\nOn a hit the token itself was never looked at again, so an **expired** token\nkept authenticating until the entry aged out.\n\nRemoved rather than bounded by the token''s `exp`. Bounding shrinks the window;\nremoving makes expiry exact on every request. What it cost is smaller than it\nlooked: the cached path still ran `Identity.objects.get(pk=...)`, so the cache\nsaved the provider call and not the database lookup. (The docstring it replaces\nclaimed it skipped both.) `AUTH_TOKEN_CACHE_TTL` goes with it rather than being\nleft as a setting that controls nothing.\n\n## The operational cost, stated per environment\n\nSigning keys are not the issue: `firebase_admin/_token_gen.py:251` wraps the\ncertificate fetch in `cachecontrol.CacheControl(requests.Session())`, held per\napp, so keys are cached per process and the steady-state per-request cost is an\nRSA verify, not an HTTPS round trip.\n\nThe uncached call is a different one. Outside `DEBUG` the default is\n`FIREBASE_SKIP_REVOCATION_CHECK=false` → `check_revoked=True` →\n`_auth_client.py:757` `get_user()` → a POST to `/accounts:lookup`, which has no\ncache. On an environment that has NOT set the flag, this commit therefore turns\nan amortised backend call into one per authenticated request. The removed cache\nwas absorbing it for 60s per token per process.\n\n`exact-staging` does set the flag, so it is unaffected. That value is Cloud Run\nconfiguration and cannot be read from this repository -- `deploy-staging.yml`\npasses no `--set-env-vars` -- so it was read from the running service:\n\n    gcloud run services describe exact-staging --region us-central1 \\\n      --format=\"value(spec.template.spec.containers[0].env)\"\n    DEBUG=false, FIREBASE_SKIP_REVOCATION_CHECK=true, ENVIRONMENT=staging\n\nThe same probe is why the LocMem claim holds: no `REDIS_URL` is set, and\n`exact/cache_config.py:65` returns LocMem without one -- so the cache being\nremoved was per-process anyway.\n\n## What this does not close\n\nWith the flag set, the provider calls `verify_id_token(check_revoked=False)`.\nThat still validates signature, issuer, audience and `exp`; what it skips is\nthe backend check for a revoked refresh token or a disabled user. So a revoked\ntoken is accepted until it expires, independently of any cache, and\n`Identity.is_active` does not compensate: it is a local flag written only by\n`accounts/models.py:61` and the admin fieldset at `accounts/admin.py:16`, with\nnothing reading Firebase''s disabled or revoked state into it.\n\nConfiguration with an owner, not a code defect: #410. The module docstring says\nso where the next reader of this code will see it, not only in the tracker.\n\n## Tests\n\n`test_every_request_is_verified_again` counts `provider.verify` calls and\nrequires two for two requests carrying the same bearer -- asserting the absence\nof the cached path directly, rather than that some cache happens to be empty.\n\nThe autouse fixture inspects LocMem''s actual keyspace for `auth:partner:` keys.\nAn earlier version of this commit asked `django_cache.get(\n\"auth:partner:sentinel\")`, which can never be non-None, because real keys are\n`auth:partner:<sha256 digest>` -- so it could not fail and did not fire even\nwith the cache fully restored. Review caught it; it is recorded because the\nfixture''s own docstring described a check the code did not perform.\n\nAlso deleted `test_deactivated_partner_identity_is_rejected_via_the_cached_path`:\nits docstring described the cache-hit branch this commit removes, and its body\nwas byte-identical to the test that replaces it.\n\nMeasured: restoring `accounts/authentication.py` from `origin/2omop` fails\n`test_every_request_is_verified_again` and trips the fixture on three tests.\nFull tests/ suite: green.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01XPMQBreB9e2A3raQjF4ch2\n(cherry picked from commit 8e623e0c3d5b3a84cbed81d830dc9ce25c57fde6)","tree":{"sha":"f417087d1ce3ce716f6392d24a46ac6cbcfc2d78","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/healthkey-ai/exact/git/trees/f417087d1ce3ce716f6392d24a46ac6cbcfc2d78"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/healthkey-ai/exact/git/commits/a4f60f7905e4cf3eeb4739e153aba06b450d6da2","verification":{"payload":"tree f417087d1ce3ce716f6392d24a46ac6cbcfc2d78\nparent 0cec006b79ab2efce6d7f715f7ec39441114b041\nauthor Leonid Morozov <leonidmorozov@icloud.com> 1788873303 +0300\ncommitter LeoMo42 <69346755+LeoMo42@users.noreply.github.com> 1789476185 +0300\n\nfix(security): remove the verified-token cache, which never re-read the token (#404)\n\n`PartnerAuthentication` cached each verification for `AUTH_TOKEN_CACHE_TTL`\n(60s by default), keyed by `SHA256(token)[:32]`, and returned it without\nconsulting any provider:\n\n    cached = self._from_cache(token)\n    if cached is not None:\n        return cached\n\nOn a hit the token itself was never looked at again, so an **expired** token\nkept authenticating until the entry aged out.\n\nRemoved rather than bounded by the token''s `exp`. Bounding shrinks the window;\nremoving makes expiry exact on every request. What it cost is smaller than it\nlooked: the cached path still ran `Identity.objects.get(pk=...)`, so the cache\nsaved the provider call and not the database lookup. (The docstring it replaces\nclaimed it skipped both.) `AUTH_TOKEN_CACHE_TTL` goes with it rather than being\nleft as a setting that controls nothing.\n\n## The operational cost, stated per environment\n\nSigning keys are not the issue: `firebase_admin/_token_gen.py:251` wraps the\ncertificate fetch in `cachecontrol.CacheControl(requests.Session())`, held per\napp, so keys are cached per process and the steady-state per-request cost is an\nRSA verify, not an HTTPS round trip.\n\nThe uncached call is a different one. Outside `DEBUG` the default is\n`FIREBASE_SKIP_REVOCATION_CHECK=false` → `check_revoked=True` →\n`_auth_client.py:757` `get_user()` → a POST to `/accounts:lookup`, which has no\ncache. On an environment that has NOT set the flag, this commit therefore turns\nan amortised backend call into one per authenticated request. The removed cache\nwas absorbing it for 60s per token per process.\n\n`exact-staging` does set the flag, so it is unaffected. That value is Cloud Run\nconfiguration and cannot be read from this repository -- `deploy-staging.yml`\npasses no `--set-env-vars` -- so it was read from the running service:\n\n    gcloud run services describe exact-staging --region us-central1 \\\n      --format=\"value(spec.template.spec.containers[0].env)\"\n    DEBUG=false, FIREBASE_SKIP_REVOCATION_CHECK=true, ENVIRONMENT=staging\n\nThe same probe is why the LocMem claim holds: no `REDIS_URL` is set, and\n`exact/cache_config.py:65` returns LocMem without one -- so the cache being\nremoved was per-process anyway.\n\n## What this does not close\n\nWith the flag set, the provider calls `verify_id_token(check_revoked=False)`.\nThat still validates signature, issuer, audience and `exp`; what it skips is\nthe backend check for a revoked refresh token or a disabled user. So a revoked\ntoken is accepted until it expires, independently of any cache, and\n`Identity.is_active` does not compensate: it is a local flag written only by\n`accounts/models.py:61` and the admin fieldset at `accounts/admin.py:16`, with\nnothing reading Firebase''s disabled or revoked state into it.\n\nConfiguration with an owner, not a code defect: #410. The module docstring says\nso where the next reader of this code will see it, not only in the tracker.\n\n## Tests\n\n`test_every_request_is_verified_again` counts `provider.verify` calls and\nrequires two for two requests carrying the same bearer -- asserting the absence\nof the cached path directly, rather than that some cache happens to be empty.\n\nThe autouse fixture inspects LocMem''s actual keyspace for `auth:partner:` keys.\nAn earlier version of this commit asked `django_cache.get(\n\"auth:partner:sentinel\")`, which can never be non-None, because real keys are\n`auth:partner:<sha256 digest>` -- so it could not fail and did not fire even\nwith the cache fully restored. Review caught it; it is recorded because the\nfixture''s own docstring described a check the code did not perform.\n\nAlso deleted `test_deactivated_partner_identity_is_rejected_via_the_cached_path`:\nits docstring described the cache-hit branch this commit removes, and its body\nwas byte-identical to the test that replaces it.\n\nMeasured: restoring `accounts/authentication.py` from `origin/2omop` fails\n`test_every_request_is_verified_again` and trips the fixture on three tests.\nFull tests/ suite: green.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01XPMQBreB9e2A3raQjF4ch2\n(cherry picked from commit 8e623e0c3d5b3a84cbed81d830dc9ce25c57fde6)\n","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN SSH SIGNATURE-----\nU1NIU0lHAAAAAQAAADMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAAAg65RgOXaobIWmShxVbQxxdrUES+\nprdck9i5CvM2zvHV4AAAADZ2l0AAAAAAAAAAZzaGE1MTIAAABTAAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5\nAAAAQFG7ljqAwBWzPXHCtdgVPh2gS2OL48lsooi/jemKvd/4oAKz+8tpbboM6JSir4VNXG\nwFfMMmQIfVuz9EzZILugc=\n-----END SSH SIGNATURE-----","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-15T13:01:39Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/69346755?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/LeoMo42","id":69346755,"login":"LeoMo42","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjY5MzQ2NzU1","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/healthkey-ai/exact/commit/a4f60f7905e4cf3eeb4739e153aba06b450d6da2","node_id":"C_kwDORYlqjNoAKGE0ZjYwZjc5MDVlNGNmM2VlYjQ3MzllMTUzYWJhMDZiNDUwZDZkYTI","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/healthkey-ai/exact/commit/0cec006b79ab2efce6d7f715f7ec39441114b041","sha":"0cec006b79ab2efce6d7f715f7ec39441114b041","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/healthkey-ai/exact/commits/0cec006b79ab2efce6d7f715f7ec39441114b041"}],"sha":"a4f60f7905e4cf3eeb4739e153aba06b450d6da2","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/healthkey-ai/exact/commits/a4f60f7905e4cf3eeb4739e153aba06b450d6da2"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/69346755?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/LeoMo42","id":69346755,"login":"LeoMo42","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjY5MzQ2NzU1","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/healthkey-ai/exact/commits/0cec006b79ab2efce6d7f715f7ec39441114b041/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-08T09:08:34Z","email":"leonidmorozov@icloud.com","name":"Leonid Morozov"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-15T12:43:05Z","email":"69346755+LeoMo42@users.noreply.github.com","name":"LeoMo42"},"message":"fix(security): attacker-controlled input 500s the auth path before verification (#405)\n\nTwo anonymous, unauthenticated 500s on the authentication chain. The issue\nnames the first; the second was found while reviewing the fix for the first and\nis fixed here rather than filed, because it is the same defect one\nauthenticator earlier and shipping half a class would be worse.\n\n## 1. A non-string `iss` (the issue as filed)\n\n    iss = unverified_payload.get(\"iss\", \"\")\n    return iss.startswith(FIREBASE_ISS_PREFIX)\n\nThe `\"\"` default covers a *missing* `iss`, not one of the wrong *type*.\n`{\"iss\": [\"x\"]}`, `5`, `{}`, `null`, `true` all reach `.startswith` on a\nnon-string and raise `AttributeError`. `can_handle` runs on\n`decode_jwt_unverified(token)` -- decoded without a signature check, by design,\nbecause routing must precede verification -- so the payload is entirely\nattacker-controlled: no key, no account, no prior request.\n\nFixed where the rule generalises: `unverified_str_claim` in providers/base.py,\nwhich every provider should use to read anything out of an unverified payload.\n\n## 2. A non-ASCII bearer token (found in review)\n\n    if not hmac.compare_digest(header[7:], secret):\n\n`compare_digest` raises `TypeError` when either str argument is non-ASCII, and\nDjango hands the header over latin-1-decoded, so any byte in 0x80-0xFF arrives\nas a non-ASCII str. `Authorization: Bearer <0xE9>` is the whole exploit.\n\nWorse-positioned than the first: `ServiceTokenAuthentication` is first in\n`DEFAULT_AUTHENTICATION_CLASSES` (exact/settings.py:291-294), so it fires\nbefore any provider routing and needs no JWT shape at all.\n\nThe comparison is on bytes, and the encoding is `latin-1` because that is the\n*inverse* of what the server did: gunicorn (`str(b, ''latin1'')`) and Django''s\nASGI handler both decode header bytes latin-1, so re-encoding latin-1\nreconstructs what the client sent. A first draft used utf-8, which\ndouble-encodes -- `b''caf\\xc3\\xa9''` becomes `b''caf\\xc3\\x83\\xc2\\xa9''` -- so a\nnon-ASCII `SERVICE_AUTH_TOKEN` would never have matched again, silently and\npermanently, with nothing in the log. That draft also justified\n`surrogatepass` with \"latin-1 decoding can leave lone surrogates\", which is\nfalse: latin-1 maps 0x00-0xFF onto U+0000-U+00FF and nothing else, so no byte\non the wire can produce one. Both are corrected, and\n`test_a_non_ascii_service_token_still_authenticates` fails if the utf-8 form\ncomes back. DRF uses the same inverse\n(`HTTP_HEADER_ENCODING = ''iso-8859-1''`).\n\nThe secret is encoded utf-8 with `surrogateescape`, matching how `os.environ`\ndecoded it, so env bytes that were not valid UTF-8 cannot raise on every\nrequest.\n\n## What neither of these is\n\nAn authentication bypass. Both raise before anything is verified, so nothing is\ngranted. The cost is availability on an anonymously reachable path, plus noise\nin the monitoring a real attack would have to be noticed in. `utf-8` with\n`surrogatepass` is injective, so no two headers could ever have compared equal.\n\n## Notes on the tests\n\nThe registry test asserts across `get_providers()` and poisons every claim the\npayload carries, not just `iss` -- a second provider routing on `aud` would\notherwise reproduce this exactly while the test stayed green. It also asserts\nthe registry is non-empty, since an empty one would make the loop assert\nnothing.\n\nThe non-ASCII cases are parametrised on what actually arrives: an HTTP header\ncarries bytes, the server decodes them latin-1, so UTF-8 Cyrillic reaches the\napplication as mojibake in 0x80-0xFF. Parametrising on the original strings\nwould only test DRF''s own header encoding, which refuses them.\n\nMeasured: 11 of the 26 tests in tests/test_auth.py fail against origin/2omop,\nall 26 pass with the change.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01XPMQBreB9e2A3raQjF4ch2\n(cherry picked from commit f23c8fd6c395d48ea00e152615b0ed12804f5d2b)","tree":{"sha":"c9a092f1ecee9aa043163257ae573cbfaac874ce","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/healthkey-ai/exact/git/trees/c9a092f1ecee9aa043163257ae573cbfaac874ce"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/healthkey-ai/exact/git/commits/0cec006b79ab2efce6d7f715f7ec39441114b041","verification":{"payload":"tree c9a092f1ecee9aa043163257ae573cbfaac874ce\nparent b21a43864e602783539820672868cca5e0900861\nauthor Leonid Morozov <leonidmorozov@icloud.com> 1788858514 +0300\ncommitter LeoMo42 <69346755+LeoMo42@users.noreply.github.com> 1789476185 +0300\n\nfix(security): attacker-controlled input 500s the auth path before verification (#405)\n\nTwo anonymous, unauthenticated 500s on the authentication chain. The issue\nnames the first; the second was found while reviewing the fix for the first and\nis fixed here rather than filed, because it is the same defect one\nauthenticator earlier and shipping half a class would be worse.\n\n## 1. A non-string `iss` (the issue as filed)\n\n    iss = unverified_payload.get(\"iss\", \"\")\n    return iss.startswith(FIREBASE_ISS_PREFIX)\n\nThe `\"\"` default covers a *missing* `iss`, not one of the wrong *type*.\n`{\"iss\": [\"x\"]}`, `5`, `{}`, `null`, `true` all reach `.startswith` on a\nnon-string and raise `AttributeError`. `can_handle` runs on\n`decode_jwt_unverified(token)` -- decoded without a signature check, by design,\nbecause routing must precede verification -- so the payload is entirely\nattacker-controlled: no key, no account, no prior request.\n\nFixed where the rule generalises: `unverified_str_claim` in providers/base.py,\nwhich every provider should use to read anything out of an unverified payload.\n\n## 2. A non-ASCII bearer token (found in review)\n\n    if not hmac.compare_digest(header[7:], secret):\n\n`compare_digest` raises `TypeError` when either str argument is non-ASCII, and\nDjango hands the header over latin-1-decoded, so any byte in 0x80-0xFF arrives\nas a non-ASCII str. `Authorization: Bearer <0xE9>` is the whole exploit.\n\nWorse-positioned than the first: `ServiceTokenAuthentication` is first in\n`DEFAULT_AUTHENTICATION_CLASSES` (exact/settings.py:291-294), so it fires\nbefore any provider routing and needs no JWT shape at all.\n\nThe comparison is on bytes, and the encoding is `latin-1` because that is the\n*inverse* of what the server did: gunicorn (`str(b, ''latin1'')`) and Django''s\nASGI handler both decode header bytes latin-1, so re-encoding latin-1\nreconstructs what the client sent. A first draft used utf-8, which\ndouble-encodes -- `b''caf\\xc3\\xa9''` becomes `b''caf\\xc3\\x83\\xc2\\xa9''` -- so a\nnon-ASCII `SERVICE_AUTH_TOKEN` would never have matched again, silently and\npermanently, with nothing in the log. That draft also justified\n`surrogatepass` with \"latin-1 decoding can leave lone surrogates\", which is\nfalse: latin-1 maps 0x00-0xFF onto U+0000-U+00FF and nothing else, so no byte\non the wire can produce one. Both are corrected, and\n`test_a_non_ascii_service_token_still_authenticates` fails if the utf-8 form\ncomes back. DRF uses the same inverse\n(`HTTP_HEADER_ENCODING = ''iso-8859-1''`).\n\nThe secret is encoded utf-8 with `surrogateescape`, matching how `os.environ`\ndecoded it, so env bytes that were not valid UTF-8 cannot raise on every\nrequest.\n\n## What neither of these is\n\nAn authentication bypass. Both raise before anything is verified, so nothing is\ngranted. The cost is availability on an anonymously reachable path, plus noise\nin the monitoring a real attack would have to be noticed in. `utf-8` with\n`surrogatepass` is injective, so no two headers could ever have compared equal.\n\n## Notes on the tests\n\nThe registry test asserts across `get_providers()` and poisons every claim the\npayload carries, not just `iss` -- a second provider routing on `aud` would\notherwise reproduce this exactly while the test stayed green. It also asserts\nthe registry is non-empty, since an empty one would make the loop assert\nnothing.\n\nThe non-ASCII cases are parametrised on what actually arrives: an HTTP header\ncarries bytes, the server decodes them latin-1, so UTF-8 Cyrillic reaches the\napplication as mojibake in 0x80-0xFF. Parametrising on the original strings\nwould only test DRF''s own header encoding, which refuses them.\n\nMeasured: 11 of the 26 tests in tests/test_auth.py fail against origin/2omop,\nall 26 pass with the change.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01XPMQBreB9e2A3raQjF4ch2\n(cherry picked from commit f23c8fd6c395d48ea00e152615b0ed12804f5d2b)\n","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN SSH SIGNATURE-----\nU1NIU0lHAAAAAQAAADMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAAAg65RgOXaobIWmShxVbQxxdrUES+\nprdck9i5CvM2zvHV4AAAADZ2l0AAAAAAAAAAZzaGE1MTIAAABTAAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5\nAAAAQApE9mA8U1vUBsfLRDClVd1SKK7i99pu5AxYabhjT7n7sdlqegV5BUxb5wr/qytzN9\nbuKDPdmRiLQFKcFRZGcw0=\n-----END SSH SIGNATURE-----","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-15T13:01:39Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/69346755?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/LeoMo42","id":69346755,"login":"LeoMo42","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjY5MzQ2NzU1","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/healthkey-ai/exact/commit/0cec006b79ab2efce6d7f715f7ec39441114b041","node_id":"C_kwDORYlqjNoAKDBjZWMwMDZiNzlhYjJlZmNlNmQ3ZjcxNWY3ZWMzOTQ0MTExNGIwNDE","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/healthkey-ai/exact/commit/b21a43864e602783539820672868cca5e0900861","sha":"b21a43864e602783539820672868cca5e0900861","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/healthkey-ai/exact/commits/b21a43864e602783539820672868cca5e0900861"}],"sha":"0cec006b79ab2efce6d7f715f7ec39441114b041","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/healthkey-ai/exact/commits/0cec006b79ab2efce6d7f715f7ec39441114b041"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/69346755?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/LeoMo42","id":69346755,"login":"LeoMo42","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjY5MzQ2NzU1","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/healthkey-ai/exact/commits/b21a43864e602783539820672868cca5e0900861/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-08T10:47:51Z","email":"leonidmorozov@icloud.com","name":"Leonid Morozov"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-15T12:42:53Z","email":"69346755+LeoMo42@users.noreply.github.com","name":"LeoMo42"},"message":"fix(security): the DB password must not reach a psql command line (#403)\n\nManagement commands and the container init scripts queried Postgres by shelling\nout to `psql` with the whole connection string -- password included -- as an\nargument:\n\n    subprocess.run([''psql'', db_url, ''-t'', ''--no-psqlrc'', ''-c'', wrapped], ...)\n    psql \"$PATIENT_DATABASE_URL\" -tAXc ''SELECT 1''\n\nCommand-line arguments are world-readable. Any local process can read\n`/proc/<pid>/cmdline`, `ps` shows argv to every user on the host, and it lands\nin shell history and in process-accounting or container-runtime logs. The\ncredential opens the patient database.\n\nlibpq takes the password out of band via `PGPASSWORD`, and `/proc/<pid>/environ`\nis owner-only. Upstream does not call `PGPASSWORD` the best answer -- the\nPostgreSQL docs note some systems let other users read a process environment,\nwhich is why #403 lists `.pgpass` and `PGSERVICEFILE` too. It is chosen because\nthe call sites already build a custom env for `PGSSLMODE`, so it needs no file\nto deploy or permission to police; the seam moves to `.pgpass` without touching\na call site if that ever changes.\n\nTwo implementations, because there are two languages:\n\n- `trials/services/psql_dsn.py` for the three management commands. Three other\n  commands import their psql helpers from `compare_trials`, so they inherit it.\n- `docker/psql_dsn.sh` for `docker/init_{patients,trials}_db.sh`, 12 psql calls\n  between them. This is the path that runs unattended on every cold start --\n  the exposure that matters more than the hand-run analysis tools.\n\n## The parsing has to match libpq, not urlsplit\n\n`urlsplit` ends the netloc at the first `#` or `?`, so for\n`postgresql://u:pa#ss@h/db` it reports a netloc of `u:pa` -- no `@`, nothing to\nstrip -- and the credentialed URL was returned untouched. libpq does not stop\nthere: verified against psql 17.2, that DSN connects to host `h` with password\n`pa#ss`. The first version of this fix therefore failed OPEN on it, and its\nown assertion agreed, because the assertion re-derived the netloc the same way.\n\nBoth now scan the raw string: authority ends at the first `/`, userinfo at the\nlast `@` of the authority. That also keeps what a component rebuild destroys --\n`.hostname` strips IPv6 brackets, turning `[2001:db8::1]:5432` into a port of\n`db8::1:5432`, and `.port` raises on libpq''s comma-separated multi-host URIs.\nBoth were working configurations.\n\nAlso handled: `?password=` and `?sslpassword=` in the query, with\npercent-decoded parameter names (libpq decodes names, so `%70assword=` is\n`password=`); keyword conninfo; and a `@` in the path, which used to truncate\nthe host in the shell version and export a bogus PGPASSWORD for a DSN that had\nno password at all.\n\nBoth implementations fail closed. The Python one raises\n`DsnStillCarriesPassword`; the shell one aborts the container start rather than\nhand psql a credentialed string while the caller''s comment says it is clean.\n\n## Care that is not obvious from the diff\n\n`urlsplit`-style components hand back the still percent-encoded password while\nlibpq wants the literal, so `unquote` is required -- without it a password\ncontaining `@ : / ?` or a space fails, and `%40` is sent as `@`. The username\nstays encoded, because it goes back into a URI and libpq decodes it itself.\n\nThe shell decode is `python3 -c urllib.parse.unquote`, not\n`printf ''%b'' \"${1//%/\\x}\"`. That expansion is not a percent decoder: it also\ninterprets backslash escapes already in the password, so `back\\slash` loses its\nbackslash, and `\\c` truncates the credential outright with no error.\n\n`psql_dsn_split` assigns rather than echoes, because `dsn=\"$(fn \"$url\")\"` runs\nin a subshell where `export PGPASSWORD` never reaches the caller -- which would\nturn a credential leak into an auth failure.\n\n## Tests\n\n52, including 12 that drive `docker/psql_dsn.sh` through bash and compare it\nagainst the Python twin field by field. Two implementations of one rule drift;\nthat is what notices.\n\nThe AST guard scans every file in the commands directory -- so a new command\nis covered the day it is written -- and flags a non-literal in any position\npsql could read as a connection string, not only argv[1]: moving one flag ahead\nof the DSN defeated the first version, and that is the shape one call site\nalready has. `TestTheGuardActuallyGuards` calls the same function the guard\nuses rather than a copy: an earlier version re-implemented it, and loosening\nthe real guard left the coverage test green.\n\nMeasured: with only the callers reverted, 4 of the 52 fail; all 52 pass with\nthe change. Full tests/ suite: green.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01XPMQBreB9e2A3raQjF4ch2\n(cherry picked from commit c2941ad68ed8ad3cc5cd285a509b421a4789baa1)","tree":{"sha":"cb988950018f7320541984080caeb5b7504a611c","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/healthkey-ai/exact/git/trees/cb988950018f7320541984080caeb5b7504a611c"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/healthkey-ai/exact/git/commits/b21a43864e602783539820672868cca5e0900861","verification":{"payload":"tree cb988950018f7320541984080caeb5b7504a611c\nparent d78aa56919c57d52c299d90cb917ee2eb1cdd0a1\nauthor Leonid Morozov <leonidmorozov@icloud.com> 1788864471 +0300\ncommitter LeoMo42 <69346755+LeoMo42@users.noreply.github.com> 1789476173 +0300\n\nfix(security): the DB password must not reach a psql command line (#403)\n\nManagement commands and the container init scripts queried Postgres by shelling\nout to `psql` with the whole connection string -- password included -- as an\nargument:\n\n    subprocess.run([''psql'', db_url, ''-t'', ''--no-psqlrc'', ''-c'', wrapped], ...)\n    psql \"$PATIENT_DATABASE_URL\" -tAXc ''SELECT 1''\n\nCommand-line arguments are world-readable. Any local process can read\n`/proc/<pid>/cmdline`, `ps` shows argv to every user on the host, and it lands\nin shell history and in process-accounting or container-runtime logs. The\ncredential opens the patient database.\n\nlibpq takes the password out of band via `PGPASSWORD`, and `/proc/<pid>/environ`\nis owner-only. Upstream does not call `PGPASSWORD` the best answer -- the\nPostgreSQL docs note some systems let other users read a process environment,\nwhich is why #403 lists `.pgpass` and `PGSERVICEFILE` too. It is chosen because\nthe call sites already build a custom env for `PGSSLMODE`, so it needs no file\nto deploy or permission to police; the seam moves to `.pgpass` without touching\na call site if that ever changes.\n\nTwo implementations, because there are two languages:\n\n- `trials/services/psql_dsn.py` for the three management commands. Three other\n  commands import their psql helpers from `compare_trials`, so they inherit it.\n- `docker/psql_dsn.sh` for `docker/init_{patients,trials}_db.sh`, 12 psql calls\n  between them. This is the path that runs unattended on every cold start --\n  the exposure that matters more than the hand-run analysis tools.\n\n## The parsing has to match libpq, not urlsplit\n\n`urlsplit` ends the netloc at the first `#` or `?`, so for\n`postgresql://u:pa#ss@h/db` it reports a netloc of `u:pa` -- no `@`, nothing to\nstrip -- and the credentialed URL was returned untouched. libpq does not stop\nthere: verified against psql 17.2, that DSN connects to host `h` with password\n`pa#ss`. The first version of this fix therefore failed OPEN on it, and its\nown assertion agreed, because the assertion re-derived the netloc the same way.\n\nBoth now scan the raw string: authority ends at the first `/`, userinfo at the\nlast `@` of the authority. That also keeps what a component rebuild destroys --\n`.hostname` strips IPv6 brackets, turning `[2001:db8::1]:5432` into a port of\n`db8::1:5432`, and `.port` raises on libpq''s comma-separated multi-host URIs.\nBoth were working configurations.\n\nAlso handled: `?password=` and `?sslpassword=` in the query, with\npercent-decoded parameter names (libpq decodes names, so `%70assword=` is\n`password=`); keyword conninfo; and a `@` in the path, which used to truncate\nthe host in the shell version and export a bogus PGPASSWORD for a DSN that had\nno password at all.\n\nBoth implementations fail closed. The Python one raises\n`DsnStillCarriesPassword`; the shell one aborts the container start rather than\nhand psql a credentialed string while the caller''s comment says it is clean.\n\n## Care that is not obvious from the diff\n\n`urlsplit`-style components hand back the still percent-encoded password while\nlibpq wants the literal, so `unquote` is required -- without it a password\ncontaining `@ : / ?` or a space fails, and `%40` is sent as `@`. The username\nstays encoded, because it goes back into a URI and libpq decodes it itself.\n\nThe shell decode is `python3 -c urllib.parse.unquote`, not\n`printf ''%b'' \"${1//%/\\x}\"`. That expansion is not a percent decoder: it also\ninterprets backslash escapes already in the password, so `back\\slash` loses its\nbackslash, and `\\c` truncates the credential outright with no error.\n\n`psql_dsn_split` assigns rather than echoes, because `dsn=\"$(fn \"$url\")\"` runs\nin a subshell where `export PGPASSWORD` never reaches the caller -- which would\nturn a credential leak into an auth failure.\n\n## Tests\n\n52, including 12 that drive `docker/psql_dsn.sh` through bash and compare it\nagainst the Python twin field by field. Two implementations of one rule drift;\nthat is what notices.\n\nThe AST guard scans every file in the commands directory -- so a new command\nis covered the day it is written -- and flags a non-literal in any position\npsql could read as a connection string, not only argv[1]: moving one flag ahead\nof the DSN defeated the first version, and that is the shape one call site\nalready has. `TestTheGuardActuallyGuards` calls the same function the guard\nuses rather than a copy: an earlier version re-implemented it, and loosening\nthe real guard left the coverage test green.\n\nMeasured: with only the callers reverted, 4 of the 52 fail; all 52 pass with\nthe change. Full tests/ suite: green.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01XPMQBreB9e2A3raQjF4ch2\n(cherry picked from commit c2941ad68ed8ad3cc5cd285a509b421a4789baa1)\n","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN SSH SIGNATURE-----\nU1NIU0lHAAAAAQAAADMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAAAg65RgOXaobIWmShxVbQxxdrUES+\nprdck9i5CvM2zvHV4AAAADZ2l0AAAAAAAAAAZzaGE1MTIAAABTAAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5\nAAAAQKMrJzazLs3yKlkO9C4VU5AKpT8df/JlVRAi6EQbcPMIq/nILPC4c4kk4EkqTp7Y44\ntPDSUXUnw0xWiNlBfqYAk=\n-----END SSH SIGNATURE-----","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-15T13:01:39Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/69346755?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/LeoMo42","id":69346755,"login":"LeoMo42","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjY5MzQ2NzU1","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/healthkey-ai/exact/commit/b21a43864e602783539820672868cca5e0900861","node_id":"C_kwDORYlqjNoAKGIyMWE0Mzg2NGU2MDI3ODM1Mzk4MjA2NzI4NjhjY2E1ZTA5MDA4NjE","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/healthkey-ai/exact/commit/d78aa56919c57d52c299d90cb917ee2eb1cdd0a1","sha":"d78aa56919c57d52c299d90cb917ee2eb1cdd0a1","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/healthkey-ai/exact/commits/d78aa56919c57d52c299d90cb917ee2eb1cdd0a1"}],"sha":"b21a43864e602783539820672868cca5e0900861","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/healthkey-ai/exact/commits/b21a43864e602783539820672868cca5e0900861"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/69346755?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/LeoMo42","id":69346755,"login":"LeoMo42","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjY5MzQ2NzU1","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/healthkey-ai/exact/commits/d78aa56919c57d52c299d90cb917ee2eb1cdd0a1/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-08T13:29:05Z","email":"leonidmorozov@icloud.com","name":"Leonid Morozov"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-15T12:42:04Z","email":"69346755+LeoMo42@users.noreply.github.com","name":"LeoMo42"},"message":"fix(security): allow-list the scheme of a server-supplied trial link (#406)\n\n`TrialDetailPage` rendered a URL from the trials corpus straight into an\nanchor -- `href={data.link}` -- with no scheme check. `data.link` is\n`trials/models.py:347` `models.TextField(default='''')`: no validator, no\nallow-list, whatever the corpus holds.\n\n## What this actually closes, on this React\n\nEXACT is on React 19.2.7, which BLOCKS a `javascript:` href outright --\n`sanitizeURL` rewrites it to `javascript:throw new Error(''React has blocked a\njavascript: URL...'')`, and the old 16.9 warning string is absent from the\nbuild. So the `javascript:` half of the finding is already handled by the\nframework here.\n\nWhat is not: `data:` and `blob:` pass through React untouched, and React never\ninspects the protocol-relative or authority-naming spellings -- `//evil`,\n`/\\evil`, `/<TAB>/evil` -- that the WHATWG parser resolves off-origin. Those\nare what this commit closes, and they are worth closing on their own.\n\nStated this way because the first draft of this message argued the fix from\nReact 18''s warn-and-render behaviour, which is CancerBot''s version, not ours.\n\n## Ported, not rewritten\n\n`frontend/src/lib/safeUrl.ts` is CancerBot''s `ui.v2/client/lib/safeUrl.ts` with\nits spec, byte-identical below a provenance header (verified by `diff`). That\nmodule exists because two hand-written copies inside CancerBot drifted\n(cancerbot-org/cancerbot#5045); a third variant here would repeat exactly that.\n\nIts two-base resolution is worth not re-deriving: a scheme-less value is\nresolved against two different bases and rejected if both give the same host,\nwhich catches authority-naming spellings without string inspection -- including\n`/<TAB>/evil.com`, where the parser strips the tab and a regex does not.\n\nTwo sentences in the ported body are CancerBot-true and EXACT-false: the React\none above, and the claim that `ftp`/`ftps` are allowed because the API stores\nthese as a DRF `URLField`. That describes CancerBot''s `StudyInfo`, not\n`Trial.link`. Both are corrected in the EXACT-only header rather than in the\nported body, so the copy stays a copy and a future diff means \"the rule\nchanged\". Whether the allow-list should narrow to http/https belongs upstream\nwhere the module lives; codex raised it, and it is not a one-repository call.\n\n`safeRedirect` comes along unused -- nothing here navigates to a\nserver-supplied URL -- for the same reason.\n\n## The call site\n\nThe anchor moved into `NctLink`, gated on the sanitised value. Gating on the\nraw one would render an `<a>` with no `href` for a rejected URL: link styling\nover something that does nothing.\n\n`safeHref` and not the stricter `safeRedirect`, whose `^https://` would fit\nthis field better except that it would drop the legacy\n`http://clinicaltrials.gov/ct2/...` links the corpus still carries. Recorded at\nthe call site, since the looser check also admits relative paths, which in a\nfederation remote resolve against the HOST application''s origin.\n\nChecked the neighbours: `SUITABILITY_HREF` is a module constant and the only\nother value reaching an `href`; no `dangerouslySetInnerHTML`, no `window.open`,\nand the one `window.location` assignment is a literal in a dev-only harness.\n\n## Tests\n\nThe ported spec (15 cases) plus `nctLink.test.tsx`, which renders the component\nwith `renderToStaticMarkup` -- no jsdom needed under this project''s\n`environment: \"node\"` -- and asserts there is NO ANCHOR for a rejected URL, not\nmerely that the href is clean.\n\nThat replaces a first version which read `TrialDetailPage.tsx` with regexes.\nReview mutated the source and showed it passed on `href={data.link || \"\"}` and\non a refactor binding the raw value to a local -- two realistic ways to\nreintroduce the finding -- while failing on three harmless rewrites. Both\nmutations now fail 14 tests each; the harmless rewrites pass.\n\nMeasured: reverting only `TrialDetailPage.tsx` fails 19 of the 65 frontend\ntests; all 65 pass with the change. `tsc --noEmit` clean.\n\nRelated: cancerbot-org/cancerbot#5289 -- migrating ui.v2 to React 19 would make\nthe React sentence true in one place rather than per directory.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01XPMQBreB9e2A3raQjF4ch2\n(cherry picked from commit 014adc2008679003f1441b0497604557196979c2)","tree":{"sha":"704d9a699b3fa6760a1e4d30dd3c50ac25a78bad","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/healthkey-ai/exact/git/trees/704d9a699b3fa6760a1e4d30dd3c50ac25a78bad"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/healthkey-ai/exact/git/commits/d78aa56919c57d52c299d90cb917ee2eb1cdd0a1","verification":{"payload":"tree 704d9a699b3fa6760a1e4d30dd3c50ac25a78bad\nparent 6f399e137c89d548ec8405addbbecc276210e293\nauthor Leonid Morozov <leonidmorozov@icloud.com> 1788874145 +0300\ncommitter LeoMo42 <69346755+LeoMo42@users.noreply.github.com> 1789476124 +0300\n\nfix(security): allow-list the scheme of a server-supplied trial link (#406)\n\n`TrialDetailPage` rendered a URL from the trials corpus straight into an\nanchor -- `href={data.link}` -- with no scheme check. `data.link` is\n`trials/models.py:347` `models.TextField(default='''')`: no validator, no\nallow-list, whatever the corpus holds.\n\n## What this actually closes, on this React\n\nEXACT is on React 19.2.7, which BLOCKS a `javascript:` href outright --\n`sanitizeURL` rewrites it to `javascript:throw new Error(''React has blocked a\njavascript: URL...'')`, and the old 16.9 warning string is absent from the\nbuild. So the `javascript:` half of the finding is already handled by the\nframework here.\n\nWhat is not: `data:` and `blob:` pass through React untouched, and React never\ninspects the protocol-relative or authority-naming spellings -- `//evil`,\n`/\\evil`, `/<TAB>/evil` -- that the WHATWG parser resolves off-origin. Those\nare what this commit closes, and they are worth closing on their own.\n\nStated this way because the first draft of this message argued the fix from\nReact 18''s warn-and-render behaviour, which is CancerBot''s version, not ours.\n\n## Ported, not rewritten\n\n`frontend/src/lib/safeUrl.ts` is CancerBot''s `ui.v2/client/lib/safeUrl.ts` with\nits spec, byte-identical below a provenance header (verified by `diff`). That\nmodule exists because two hand-written copies inside CancerBot drifted\n(cancerbot-org/cancerbot#5045); a third variant here would repeat exactly that.\n\nIts two-base resolution is worth not re-deriving: a scheme-less value is\nresolved against two different bases and rejected if both give the same host,\nwhich catches authority-naming spellings without string inspection -- including\n`/<TAB>/evil.com`, where the parser strips the tab and a regex does not.\n\nTwo sentences in the ported body are CancerBot-true and EXACT-false: the React\none above, and the claim that `ftp`/`ftps` are allowed because the API stores\nthese as a DRF `URLField`. That describes CancerBot''s `StudyInfo`, not\n`Trial.link`. Both are corrected in the EXACT-only header rather than in the\nported body, so the copy stays a copy and a future diff means \"the rule\nchanged\". Whether the allow-list should narrow to http/https belongs upstream\nwhere the module lives; codex raised it, and it is not a one-repository call.\n\n`safeRedirect` comes along unused -- nothing here navigates to a\nserver-supplied URL -- for the same reason.\n\n## The call site\n\nThe anchor moved into `NctLink`, gated on the sanitised value. Gating on the\nraw one would render an `<a>` with no `href` for a rejected URL: link styling\nover something that does nothing.\n\n`safeHref` and not the stricter `safeRedirect`, whose `^https://` would fit\nthis field better except that it would drop the legacy\n`http://clinicaltrials.gov/ct2/...` links the corpus still carries. Recorded at\nthe call site, since the looser check also admits relative paths, which in a\nfederation remote resolve against the HOST application''s origin.\n\nChecked the neighbours: `SUITABILITY_HREF` is a module constant and the only\nother value reaching an `href`; no `dangerouslySetInnerHTML`, no `window.open`,\nand the one `window.location` assignment is a literal in a dev-only harness.\n\n## Tests\n\nThe ported spec (15 cases) plus `nctLink.test.tsx`, which renders the component\nwith `renderToStaticMarkup` -- no jsdom needed under this project''s\n`environment: \"node\"` -- and asserts there is NO ANCHOR for a rejected URL, not\nmerely that the href is clean.\n\nThat replaces a first version which read `TrialDetailPage.tsx` with regexes.\nReview mutated the source and showed it passed on `href={data.link || \"\"}` and\non a refactor binding the raw value to a local -- two realistic ways to\nreintroduce the finding -- while failing on three harmless rewrites. Both\nmutations now fail 14 tests each; the harmless rewrites pass.\n\nMeasured: reverting only `TrialDetailPage.tsx` fails 19 of the 65 frontend\ntests; all 65 pass with the change. `tsc --noEmit` clean.\n\nRelated: cancerbot-org/cancerbot#5289 -- migrating ui.v2 to React 19 would make\nthe React sentence true in one place rather than per directory.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01XPMQBreB9e2A3raQjF4ch2\n(cherry picked from commit 014adc2008679003f1441b0497604557196979c2)\n","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN SSH SIGNATURE-----\nU1NIU0lHAAAAAQAAADMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAAAg65RgOXaobIWmShxVbQxxdrUES+\nprdck9i5CvM2zvHV4AAAADZ2l0AAAAAAAAAAZzaGE1MTIAAABTAAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5\nAAAAQI0UqmaFRsNwEnlpM5vGxCWKlThFmTpyGDp1dm95BA6lA7u6K1J/HmOD+MTT9PQU2S\nrQLJwLnD7SMFcD4nMjdAU=\n-----END SSH SIGNATURE-----","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-15T13:01:39Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/69346755?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/LeoMo42","id":69346755,"login":"LeoMo42","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjY5MzQ2NzU1","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/healthkey-ai/exact/commit/d78aa56919c57d52c299d90cb917ee2eb1cdd0a1","node_id":"C_kwDORYlqjNoAKGQ3OGFhNTY5MTljNTdkNTJjMjk5ZDkwY2I5MTdlZTJlYjFjZGQwYTE","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/healthkey-ai/exact/commit/6f399e137c89d548ec8405addbbecc276210e293","sha":"6f399e137c89d548ec8405addbbecc276210e293","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/healthkey-ai/exact/commits/6f399e137c89d548ec8405addbbecc276210e293"}],"sha":"d78aa56919c57d52c299d90cb917ee2eb1cdd0a1","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/healthkey-ai/exact/commits/d78aa56919c57d52c299d90cb917ee2eb1cdd0a1"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/2562446?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeonidMorozov/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeonidMorozov/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeonidMorozov/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeonidMorozov/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/LeonidMorozov","id":2562446,"login":"LeonidMorozov","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjI1NjI0NDY=","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeonidMorozov/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeonidMorozov/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeonidMorozov/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeonidMorozov/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeonidMorozov/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeonidMorozov","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/healthkey-ai/exact/commits/6f399e137c89d548ec8405addbbecc276210e293/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-08-30T18:31:05Z","email":"lmmobi98@gmail.com","name":"Leonid Morozov"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-15T12:42:04Z","email":"69346755+LeoMo42@users.noreply.github.com","name":"LeoMo42"},"message":"fix(auth): honor Identity.is_active in the house auth backends (#397 follow-up)\n\n`IsAuthenticated` only consults `is_authenticated`, which `AbstractBaseUser`\nhardcodes to True. Neither `PartnerAuthentication` nor\n`ServiceTokenAuthentication` read `Identity.is_active`, so unticking the admin''s\n`is_active` box revoked nothing: an offboarded partner kept full access to every\n`/trials/*` endpoint until Firebase itself revoked the token, and the operator\ngot no signal that the control had done nothing. DRF''s own backends reject\ninactive users (rest_framework/authentication.py:103,205) — these two were the\nexception.\n\nReject a deactivated Identity in both backends via `_reject_if_inactive`,\nincluding on the verified-token cache path, so a deactivation bites immediately\nrather than after `AUTH_TOKEN_CACHE_TTL`.\n\nThree regression tests, each verified to fail without this change: the service\nidentity, the partner identity, and the cache-hit path (which asserts the second\nrequest never re-entered `provider.verify`, so it genuinely exercises the cached\nbranch).\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01PuGkd1Cs4w1uCDFYKdVwJZ\n(cherry picked from commit 249a0c54a5a6ffee6e74303807dc9a24b4b97c95)","tree":{"sha":"98f507cc28d2d2f3a2e541b0c4afc793efcb0b03","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/healthkey-ai/exact/git/trees/98f507cc28d2d2f3a2e541b0c4afc793efcb0b03"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/healthkey-ai/exact/git/commits/6f399e137c89d548ec8405addbbecc276210e293","verification":{"payload":"tree 98f507cc28d2d2f3a2e541b0c4afc793efcb0b03\nparent 4a3c26f2badc4bbcf657f99bd46cbd184e28e199\nauthor Leonid Morozov <lmmobi98@gmail.com> 1788114665 +0300\ncommitter LeoMo42 <69346755+LeoMo42@users.noreply.github.com> 1789476124 +0300\n\nfix(auth): honor Identity.is_active in the house auth backends (#397 follow-up)\n\n`IsAuthenticated` only consults `is_authenticated`, which `AbstractBaseUser`\nhardcodes to True. Neither `PartnerAuthentication` nor\n`ServiceTokenAuthentication` read `Identity.is_active`, so unticking the admin''s\n`is_active` box revoked nothing: an offboarded partner kept full access to every\n`/trials/*` endpoint until Firebase itself revoked the token, and the operator\ngot no signal that the control had done nothing. DRF''s own backends reject\ninactive users (rest_framework/authentication.py:103,205) — these two were the\nexception.\n\nReject a deactivated Identity in both backends via `_reject_if_inactive`,\nincluding on the verified-token cache path, so a deactivation bites immediately\nrather than after `AUTH_TOKEN_CACHE_TTL`.\n\nThree regression tests, each verified to fail without this change: the service\nidentity, the partner identity, and the cache-hit path (which asserts the second\nrequest never re-entered `provider.verify`, so it genuinely exercises the cached\nbranch).\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01PuGkd1Cs4w1uCDFYKdVwJZ\n(cherry picked from commit 249a0c54a5a6ffee6e74303807dc9a24b4b97c95)\n","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN SSH SIGNATURE-----\nU1NIU0lHAAAAAQAAADMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAAAg65RgOXaobIWmShxVbQxxdrUES+\nprdck9i5CvM2zvHV4AAAADZ2l0AAAAAAAAAAZzaGE1MTIAAABTAAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5\nAAAAQKE9LcediJ0dGE5xfXXPDrWgS3XcwjS3wZm7vfOHut8XUBOQJnLsEgTA6cB38aIfKv\nbNrHfkmYdhePBgDgQsQAU=\n-----END SSH SIGNATURE-----","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-15T13:01:39Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/69346755?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/LeoMo42","id":69346755,"login":"LeoMo42","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjY5MzQ2NzU1","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/healthkey-ai/exact/commit/6f399e137c89d548ec8405addbbecc276210e293","node_id":"C_kwDORYlqjNoAKDZmMzk5ZTEzN2M4OWQ1NDhlYzg0MDVhZGRiYmVjYzI3NjIxMGUyOTM","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/healthkey-ai/exact/commit/4a3c26f2badc4bbcf657f99bd46cbd184e28e199","sha":"4a3c26f2badc4bbcf657f99bd46cbd184e28e199","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/healthkey-ai/exact/commits/4a3c26f2badc4bbcf657f99bd46cbd184e28e199"}],"sha":"6f399e137c89d548ec8405addbbecc276210e293","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/healthkey-ai/exact/commits/6f399e137c89d548ec8405addbbecc276210e293"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/2562446?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeonidMorozov/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeonidMorozov/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeonidMorozov/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeonidMorozov/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/LeonidMorozov","id":2562446,"login":"LeonidMorozov","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjI1NjI0NDY=","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeonidMorozov/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeonidMorozov/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeonidMorozov/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeonidMorozov/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeonidMorozov/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeonidMorozov","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/healthkey-ai/exact/commits/4a3c26f2badc4bbcf657f99bd46cbd184e28e199/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-08-30T18:31:05Z","email":"lmmobi98@gmail.com","name":"Leonid Morozov"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-15T12:42:04Z","email":"69346755+LeoMo42@users.noreply.github.com","name":"LeoMo42"},"message":"fix(docker): a failed DB probe must not authorise DROP SCHEMA (#397)\n\nThe restore guard read\n\n    row_count=\"$(psql ... 2>/dev/null || true)\"\n\nwhich collapsed every failure mode — unreachable host, wrong credentials,\nmissing SELECT grant, renamed table — into an empty string. That empty string\nfailed the `^[0-9]+$` test and fell through to\n`DROP SCHEMA IF EXISTS public CASCADE`, destroying a database that may have been\nfully populated. The guard written to prevent exactly that was the thing that\nfailed open.\n\nProbe in steps instead, aborting on any probe failure:\n\n  1. connectivity, surfacing psql''s own error rather than swallowing it —\n     collapsing distinct failures into one opaque message is the bug being\n     fixed here, so the diagnostic is the point;\n  2. `to_regclass(''public.<table>'')` for existence — chosen over error-string\n     matching so the legitimate fresh-database path keeps working;\n  3. the row count, schema-qualified to `public`.\n\nOnly a probe that succeeds and genuinely reports \"no table\" or \"zero rows\" now\nauthorises the restore.\n\nBoth queries target `public`, the schema the restore actually drops. Leaving the\ncount to resolve through `search_path` would let an empty same-named table in\nanother schema authorise dropping a populated `public`. The mirror case is\nguarded too: if the table is absent from `public` but resolves elsewhere via\n`search_path`, this is not the fresh database it appears to be, so refuse rather\nthan drop a schema we never inspected.\n\nVerified against a real PostgreSQL 17 under bash 5 (the scripts use `${VAR,,}`,\nso bash 4+):\n\n  fresh, table absent                       -> proceed to restore\n  populated                                 -> skip, exit 0\n  unreachable host                          -> refuse, exit 1 (+ psql''s error)\n  table present, SELECT revoked             -> refuse, exit 1\n  search_path=alt (empty), public populated -> skip, exit 0\n  table only outside public                 -> refuse, exit 1\n\nThe pre-change script reached the DROP in the unreachable-host case, and an\nintermediate revision of this patch reached it in the search_path case.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01PuGkd1Cs4w1uCDFYKdVwJZ\n(cherry picked from commit e514478c60f042deb84b0f879cbbc35185b9e448)","tree":{"sha":"e8b133979f307e854e3ac45a831c0c16c0a563ad","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/healthkey-ai/exact/git/trees/e8b133979f307e854e3ac45a831c0c16c0a563ad"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/healthkey-ai/exact/git/commits/4a3c26f2badc4bbcf657f99bd46cbd184e28e199","verification":{"payload":"tree e8b133979f307e854e3ac45a831c0c16c0a563ad\nparent 0647513f7809eaa2f3ce9ad6538f910cf3e2ed19\nauthor Leonid Morozov <lmmobi98@gmail.com> 1788114665 +0300\ncommitter LeoMo42 <69346755+LeoMo42@users.noreply.github.com> 1789476124 +0300\n\nfix(docker): a failed DB probe must not authorise DROP SCHEMA (#397)\n\nThe restore guard read\n\n    row_count=\"$(psql ... 2>/dev/null || true)\"\n\nwhich collapsed every failure mode — unreachable host, wrong credentials,\nmissing SELECT grant, renamed table — into an empty string. That empty string\nfailed the `^[0-9]+$` test and fell through to\n`DROP SCHEMA IF EXISTS public CASCADE`, destroying a database that may have been\nfully populated. The guard written to prevent exactly that was the thing that\nfailed open.\n\nProbe in steps instead, aborting on any probe failure:\n\n  1. connectivity, surfacing psql''s own error rather than swallowing it —\n     collapsing distinct failures into one opaque message is the bug being\n     fixed here, so the diagnostic is the point;\n  2. `to_regclass(''public.<table>'')` for existence — chosen over error-string\n     matching so the legitimate fresh-database path keeps working;\n  3. the row count, schema-qualified to `public`.\n\nOnly a probe that succeeds and genuinely reports \"no table\" or \"zero rows\" now\nauthorises the restore.\n\nBoth queries target `public`, the schema the restore actually drops. Leaving the\ncount to resolve through `search_path` would let an empty same-named table in\nanother schema authorise dropping a populated `public`. The mirror case is\nguarded too: if the table is absent from `public` but resolves elsewhere via\n`search_path`, this is not the fresh database it appears to be, so refuse rather\nthan drop a schema we never inspected.\n\nVerified against a real PostgreSQL 17 under bash 5 (the scripts use `${VAR,,}`,\nso bash 4+):\n\n  fresh, table absent                       -> proceed to restore\n  populated                                 -> skip, exit 0\n  unreachable host                          -> refuse, exit 1 (+ psql''s error)\n  table present, SELECT revoked             -> refuse, exit 1\n  search_path=alt (empty), public populated -> skip, exit 0\n  table only outside public                 -> refuse, exit 1\n\nThe pre-change script reached the DROP in the unreachable-host case, and an\nintermediate revision of this patch reached it in the search_path case.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01PuGkd1Cs4w1uCDFYKdVwJZ\n(cherry picked from commit e514478c60f042deb84b0f879cbbc35185b9e448)\n","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN SSH SIGNATURE-----\nU1NIU0lHAAAAAQAAADMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAAAg65RgOXaobIWmShxVbQxxdrUES+\nprdck9i5CvM2zvHV4AAAADZ2l0AAAAAAAAAAZzaGE1MTIAAABTAAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5\nAAAAQJjTgGfghujW9Ae0UKela+SieArcDUaT4bxoJzRQ4j2IKskwyPGNYylbvKul2lQ9Dx\n12TlW+27uaAdiJP6H4WgM=\n-----END SSH SIGNATURE-----","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-15T13:01:39Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/69346755?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/LeoMo42","id":69346755,"login":"LeoMo42","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjY5MzQ2NzU1","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/healthkey-ai/exact/commit/4a3c26f2badc4bbcf657f99bd46cbd184e28e199","node_id":"C_kwDORYlqjNoAKDRhM2MyNmYyYmFkYzRiYmNmNjU3Zjk5YmQ0NmNiZDE4NGUyOGUxOTk","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/healthkey-ai/exact/commit/0647513f7809eaa2f3ce9ad6538f910cf3e2ed19","sha":"0647513f7809eaa2f3ce9ad6538f910cf3e2ed19","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/healthkey-ai/exact/commits/0647513f7809eaa2f3ce9ad6538f910cf3e2ed19"}],"sha":"4a3c26f2badc4bbcf657f99bd46cbd184e28e199","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/healthkey-ai/exact/commits/4a3c26f2badc4bbcf657f99bd46cbd184e28e199"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/2562446?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeonidMorozov/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeonidMorozov/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeonidMorozov/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeonidMorozov/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/LeonidMorozov","id":2562446,"login":"LeonidMorozov","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjI1NjI0NDY=","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeonidMorozov/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeonidMorozov/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeonidMorozov/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeonidMorozov/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeonidMorozov/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeonidMorozov","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/healthkey-ai/exact/commits/0c7a5f71811edad09711d4a4743555a776984525/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-15T10:30:03Z","email":"lmmobi98@gmail.com","name":"Leonid Morozov"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-15T12:39:48Z","email":"69346755+LeoMo42@users.noreply.github.com","name":"LeoMo42"},"message":"fix(tp53): neither side of a contradiction may be decided silently\n\nAddresses the blocking points on #488. The port itself is sound — single choke\npoint, faithful cherry-pick, every line covered by a test that dies when\nreverted — so this changes only what review asked for. Two cells of the\nprecedence table move; the other six are byte-identical to #488.\n\n                    derivation=True      derivation=False\n    key absent      True                 False            (unchanged)\n    explicit null   None -> True         None             (changed)\n    explicit true   True                 True             (unchanged)\n    explicit false  False -> None        False            (changed)\n\n## 1. An unresolved aggregate cannot retract evidence in the same payload\n\nA patient with documented `del17p13` + `tp53Mutation` stopped being excluded\nfrom a trial whose protocol EXCLUDES TP53-disrupted patients:\n\n    markers + key absent (dev today)  ->  True  ->  vs EXCLUDING trial: not_matched\n    markers + explicit null (#488)    ->  None  ->  vs EXCLUDING trial: unknown\n\n`unknown` leaves the trial standing as a candidate. Same for the IHC surrogate\n(`p53_ihc >= 50`), which exists here and NOT on `main` — so the blast radius on\n`dev` is larger than the branch #488 was validated against.\n\n#488''s own description says the aggregate is `true` or `null`. If PROMOP never\nemits `false`, `null` is its \"not computed\", not a clinician''s \"unknown\" — and\na source that did not compute the aggregate cannot retract findings carried in\nthe same payload.\n\n## 2. An explicit `false` over positive markers is a contradiction, not a negative\n\nThis cell is inherited from #488 rather than introduced by it, and an earlier\nrevision of THIS commit kept it, on the reasoning that a caller asserting a\nvalue differs from a caller having none. That reasoning does not survive the\nfacts: `tp53_disruption` is a field EXACT DERIVES (`normalize.py:48`), and\n`PatientInfoSerializer.to_representation` emits it verbatim. Measured — a\nrecord with no markers serialises `tp53_disruption: False`, so a caller that\nreads a patient record and posts it back carries EXACT''s own derivation on the\nwire, where it silenced the markers:\n\n    {''molecular_markers'': ''tp53Mutation'', ''tp53_disruption'': <echoed False>}\n    before: False        now: None\n\n`None` dominates `False` in both directions of harm. Against a trial that\nEXCLUDES TP53 disruption, `False` reads as `matched` and offers the trial\noutright; against one that REQUIRES it, `False` reads as `not_matched` and\ndenies a patient their own markers qualify. `None` reads as `unknown` in both:\nthe question stays open instead of being answered wrongly.\n\nWith no contrary evidence an explicit `false` still confirms a negative — the\nordinary case is untouched.\n\nOne asymmetry stated plainly rather than left implied: the echo argument is\nsymmetric for `true`, which is equally EXACT''s own derivation coming back, yet\nan explicit `true` still wins unconditionally. That rests on the second leg\nalone — a positive marker is strong evidence, its ABSENCE is weak — and it is\ninherited from #488 unchanged, so it is not decided here. Worth revisiting with\nthe sibling fields below.\n\n## 3. The raise: right answer, wrong scope\n\n`ValidationError` on a non-boolean is kept for the ONE caller that is a client\nand dropped for the two that are not. Strictness is a property of the HTTP\nboundary, not of a builder:\n\n    resolve_patient_info (inline payload)     strict=True   -> 400\n    build_patient_info_from_ctomop_row        strict=False  -> log + derive\n    four management commands                  strict=False  -> log + derive\n\nRemoving it outright was the first attempt here, and a second reviewer was\nright to refuse that: a client sending `\"false\"` would silently get an answer\nderived from markers, possibly the opposite one. Its reasoning was wrong — the\n400 is one commit old, not an established contract, and on `dev` these values\nalready fell through — but the objection stands on its own.\n\nKeeping it everywhere was wrong too. `_build_in_memory` is shared, so on the\nother two paths the value came from UPSTREAM and a raise blames the wrong\nparty: `trials_views._resolve_patient_info` re-raises `APIException` unchanged\nwhile its own comment says a person_id-path failure must surface as 500\n\"rather than masking it as a misleading 400\", and in a batch command it is not\na 400 at all but a traceback that ends the run. It also broke the contract the\nfunction documents and implements next door — `_coerce_dates`,\n`_coerce_numerics` (\"CB API can send \\\"10.20\\\" etc.\") and `_coerce_json_fields`\nall accept loose input, and the module docstring says \"CancerBot depends on\nthis contract — do not change\".\n\n`strict` defaults OFF so a future caller has to opt into raising, and the error\nis namespaced under `patient_info` to match the sibling errors on that path.\n\n## Tests\n\n19 -> 35. The five `test_non_boolean_aggregate_is_rejected` cases now assert\nthe upstream fallback, and five more assert the client still gets its 400; the rest cover the two moved cells, including\nthe serializer round trip end to end. The gap that let this through was that\nmarker-positive-plus-explicit-aggregate had NO coverage at all, which is why\n19 green tests missed both inversions.\n\nMutation-checked, `--create-db` throughout:\n* restoring `null` wins unconditionally -> 4 failures, nothing else\n* restoring the contradiction resolved as `False` -> 4 failures, nothing else\n* making every caller strict -> 6 failures\n* making no caller strict -> 5 failures\n\nOne existing test changed: `test_inline_patient_info_wins_over_person_id`\nasserts the call SHAPE, and the shape gained `strict=True` by intent.\n\n1123 passed, 6 skipped.\n\n## Deliberately NOT in this PR\n\nFive sibling fields have the same defect — explicit `null` in, confirmed\nnegative out: `renal_adequacy_status`, `hepatic_adequacy_status`,\n`haematological_adequacy_status`, `tnbc_status`, `metastatic_status`. A shared\n`pi._provided_fields = set(filtered)` would close the category for about the\nsame lines. Left out because it changes clinical behaviour in five more places\nand deserves its own decision rather than riding along inside a review fix.","tree":{"sha":"2f0116d6aab033aea909ea873ead7c59f86b8dbe","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/healthkey-ai/exact/git/trees/2f0116d6aab033aea909ea873ead7c59f86b8dbe"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/healthkey-ai/exact/git/commits/0c7a5f71811edad09711d4a4743555a776984525","verification":{"payload":"tree 2f0116d6aab033aea909ea873ead7c59f86b8dbe\nparent bbbbe449574ffdcffd3bd608fc41c31f977fed8d\nauthor Leonid Morozov <lmmobi98@gmail.com> 1789468203 +0300\ncommitter LeoMo42 <69346755+LeoMo42@users.noreply.github.com> 1789475988 +0300\n\nfix(tp53): neither side of a contradiction may be decided silently\n\nAddresses the blocking points on #488. The port itself is sound — single choke\npoint, faithful cherry-pick, every line covered by a test that dies when\nreverted — so this changes only what review asked for. Two cells of the\nprecedence table move; the other six are byte-identical to #488.\n\n                    derivation=True      derivation=False\n    key absent      True                 False            (unchanged)\n    explicit null   None -> True         None             (changed)\n    explicit true   True                 True             (unchanged)\n    explicit false  False -> None        False            (changed)\n\n## 1. An unresolved aggregate cannot retract evidence in the same payload\n\nA patient with documented `del17p13` + `tp53Mutation` stopped being excluded\nfrom a trial whose protocol EXCLUDES TP53-disrupted patients:\n\n    markers + key absent (dev today)  ->  True  ->  vs EXCLUDING trial: not_matched\n    markers + explicit null (#488)    ->  None  ->  vs EXCLUDING trial: unknown\n\n`unknown` leaves the trial standing as a candidate. Same for the IHC surrogate\n(`p53_ihc >= 50`), which exists here and NOT on `main` — so the blast radius on\n`dev` is larger than the branch #488 was validated against.\n\n#488''s own description says the aggregate is `true` or `null`. If PROMOP never\nemits `false`, `null` is its \"not computed\", not a clinician''s \"unknown\" — and\na source that did not compute the aggregate cannot retract findings carried in\nthe same payload.\n\n## 2. An explicit `false` over positive markers is a contradiction, not a negative\n\nThis cell is inherited from #488 rather than introduced by it, and an earlier\nrevision of THIS commit kept it, on the reasoning that a caller asserting a\nvalue differs from a caller having none. That reasoning does not survive the\nfacts: `tp53_disruption` is a field EXACT DERIVES (`normalize.py:48`), and\n`PatientInfoSerializer.to_representation` emits it verbatim. Measured — a\nrecord with no markers serialises `tp53_disruption: False`, so a caller that\nreads a patient record and posts it back carries EXACT''s own derivation on the\nwire, where it silenced the markers:\n\n    {''molecular_markers'': ''tp53Mutation'', ''tp53_disruption'': <echoed False>}\n    before: False        now: None\n\n`None` dominates `False` in both directions of harm. Against a trial that\nEXCLUDES TP53 disruption, `False` reads as `matched` and offers the trial\noutright; against one that REQUIRES it, `False` reads as `not_matched` and\ndenies a patient their own markers qualify. `None` reads as `unknown` in both:\nthe question stays open instead of being answered wrongly.\n\nWith no contrary evidence an explicit `false` still confirms a negative — the\nordinary case is untouched.\n\nOne asymmetry stated plainly rather than left implied: the echo argument is\nsymmetric for `true`, which is equally EXACT''s own derivation coming back, yet\nan explicit `true` still wins unconditionally. That rests on the second leg\nalone — a positive marker is strong evidence, its ABSENCE is weak — and it is\ninherited from #488 unchanged, so it is not decided here. Worth revisiting with\nthe sibling fields below.\n\n## 3. The raise: right answer, wrong scope\n\n`ValidationError` on a non-boolean is kept for the ONE caller that is a client\nand dropped for the two that are not. Strictness is a property of the HTTP\nboundary, not of a builder:\n\n    resolve_patient_info (inline payload)     strict=True   -> 400\n    build_patient_info_from_ctomop_row        strict=False  -> log + derive\n    four management commands                  strict=False  -> log + derive\n\nRemoving it outright was the first attempt here, and a second reviewer was\nright to refuse that: a client sending `\"false\"` would silently get an answer\nderived from markers, possibly the opposite one. Its reasoning was wrong — the\n400 is one commit old, not an established contract, and on `dev` these values\nalready fell through — but the objection stands on its own.\n\nKeeping it everywhere was wrong too. `_build_in_memory` is shared, so on the\nother two paths the value came from UPSTREAM and a raise blames the wrong\nparty: `trials_views._resolve_patient_info` re-raises `APIException` unchanged\nwhile its own comment says a person_id-path failure must surface as 500\n\"rather than masking it as a misleading 400\", and in a batch command it is not\na 400 at all but a traceback that ends the run. It also broke the contract the\nfunction documents and implements next door — `_coerce_dates`,\n`_coerce_numerics` (\"CB API can send \\\"10.20\\\" etc.\") and `_coerce_json_fields`\nall accept loose input, and the module docstring says \"CancerBot depends on\nthis contract — do not change\".\n\n`strict` defaults OFF so a future caller has to opt into raising, and the error\nis namespaced under `patient_info` to match the sibling errors on that path.\n\n## Tests\n\n19 -> 35. The five `test_non_boolean_aggregate_is_rejected` cases now assert\nthe upstream fallback, and five more assert the client still gets its 400; the rest cover the two moved cells, including\nthe serializer round trip end to end. The gap that let this through was that\nmarker-positive-plus-explicit-aggregate had NO coverage at all, which is why\n19 green tests missed both inversions.\n\nMutation-checked, `--create-db` throughout:\n* restoring `null` wins unconditionally -> 4 failures, nothing else\n* restoring the contradiction resolved as `False` -> 4 failures, nothing else\n* making every caller strict -> 6 failures\n* making no caller strict -> 5 failures\n\nOne existing test changed: `test_inline_patient_info_wins_over_person_id`\nasserts the call SHAPE, and the shape gained `strict=True` by intent.\n\n1123 passed, 6 skipped.\n\n## Deliberately NOT in this PR\n\nFive sibling fields have the same defect — explicit `null` in, confirmed\nnegative out: `renal_adequacy_status`, `hepatic_adequacy_status`,\n`haematological_adequacy_status`, `tnbc_status`, `metastatic_status`. A shared\n`pi._provided_fields = set(filtered)` would close the category for about the\nsame lines. Left out because it changes clinical behaviour in five more places\nand deserves its own decision rather than riding along inside a review fix.\n","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN SSH SIGNATURE-----\nU1NIU0lHAAAAAQAAADMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAAAg65RgOXaobIWmShxVbQxxdrUES+\nprdck9i5CvM2zvHV4AAAADZ2l0AAAAAAAAAAZzaGE1MTIAAABTAAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5\nAAAAQO6z/nynPjcOuEZzGG1kWvNtiQWHM3oTU4dsGsiO2gR+ha5Wubn3vvv7fnqbAqDZvr\n3nMCG29iOu/d6xhlzjog8=\n-----END SSH SIGNATURE-----","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-15T12:41:47Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/69346755?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/LeoMo42/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/us